/* Sarum Mass propers of the seasons, from F. E. Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911, public domain), Part I. Built from a scan. */
self.SARUM_MASS={"adv1":{"title":"Mass of the First Sunday in Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 67","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul; my God, I have put my trust in thee: O let me not be confounded, neither let mine enemies triumph over me.\nPs. Shew me thy ways, O Lord: and teach me thy paths."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Stir up, we beseech thee, O Lord thy power, and come; that we may be found worthy to be rescued by thy protection from the threatening dangers of our sins, and to be set free by thy deliverance. Who livest and reignest with God the Father in the unity of the Holy Spirit, God for ever and ever. Amen."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xiii. 11–14","inc":"And that knowing the time … the Lord Jesus Christ.","ref":"romans 13:11-14"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"For all they that hope in thee shall not be ashamed, O Lord.\n℣. Shew me thy ways, O Lord, and teach me thy paths."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Thou for ever our salvation,\nthou the life of all creation,\nthou our hope of restoration,\nthou the never-failing light;\nGrieving for man's loss impending,\nby the tempter's wiles pretending,\ncamest down thine aid extending,\nleaving not the starry height.\nIn our flesh thy glory veiling,\nall on earth, in ruin failing,\nthou didst save by might prevailing,\nbringing joy to all our race.\nGrant, O Christ, thine expiation,\nunto us thine own creation,\ntake us for an habitation\ncleansed for thyself to grace.\nBy thy first humiliation\ngrant us. Lord, justification;\nwhen again in exaltation\nthou shalt come, O set us free.\nWhen in glory manifested\nthou the secret heart hast tested,\nin unsullied robes invested\nmay we closely follow thee!"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat., xxi. 1–9","inc":"And when Jesus drew nigh … Hosanna in the highest.","ref":"matthew 21:1-9"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul; my God, I have put my trust in thee: O let me not be confounded, neither let mine enemies triumph over me, for all they that hope in thee shall not be ashamed.\n℣. Lead me forth in thy truth and learn me; for thou art the God of my salvation; in thee hath been my hope all the day long.\n℣. Look upon me and have mercy upon me; O Lord, keep my soul and deliver me, because I have put my trust in thee."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May these sacred gifts, O Lord, cleanse us by their powerful virtue, and make to us come with more purity to him who gave them. Through."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord shall shew lovingkindness, and our land shall give her increase."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May we receive, O Lord, thy mercy in the midst of thy temple, and anticipate with due honours the approaching solemnity of our restoration. Through."}]},"adv1-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in the first week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 77","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James v. 7–10","inc":"Be patient, therefore … of patience.","ref":"james 5:7-10"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark i. 1–8","inc":"The beginning … the Holy Ghost.","ref":"mark 1:1-8"}]},"adv1-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday in the first week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 77","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. li. 1–8","inc":"Hearken to me … to generation.","ref":"isaiah 51:1-8"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. iii. 1–6","inc":"In those days … confessing their sins.","ref":"matthew 3:1-6"}]},"adv2":{"title":"Mass of the Second Sunday in Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 78","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O people of Sion, behold the Lord shall come to save the nations, and the Lord shall make his glorious voice to be heard in the gladness of your heart.\nPs. Hear, O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Stir up, O Lord, our hearts to prepare the way of thy only-begotten One, that through his coming we may be found worthy to serve thee with pure hearts, who liveth and reigneth with thee in the unity of the Holy Spirit, God for ever and ever."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xv. 4–13","inc":"Brethren, whatsoever things … of the Holy Ghost.","ref":"romans 15:4-13"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Out of Sion hath God appeared in perfect beauty. Our God shall come and shall not keep silence."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let the choir devoutly bring\nWelcome\nAnd with to\none th'consent\neternal renew\nKing,\nThe Creator's homage due.\nHim angelic legions praise,\nOn his face enraptured gaze.\nOn him wait all earthly things\nTill his nod their trial brings.\nAwful he in judgements deep\nYet in might doth mercy keep;\nBy thine agony of woe\nPity, Lord, and save us now.\nTo the gleaming stars on high\nRaise the world in purity:\nLet thy saving health appear\nScattering perils far and near.\nBid the universe be clean,\nLet us live in peace serene.\nTill unto those realms we soar\nWhere thou reignest evermore."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xxi. 25–33","inc":"There shall be signs … not pass away.","ref":"luke 21:25-33"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Wilt thou not turn again and quicken us that the people may rejoice in thee? Shew us thy mercy, O Lord, and grant us thy salvation.\n℣. Lord, thou art become gracious unto thy land: thou hast turned away the captivity of Jacob: thou hast forgiven the offence of thy people.\n℣. Mercy and truth are met together. Truth shall flourish out of the earth, and righteousness hath looked down from heaven."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Be pleased, O Lord, we beseech to accept our humble prayers and oblations, and whereas our merits avail nothing, succour us with thy protection. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Arise, O Jerusalem, and stand on high, and behold the joy that Cometh unto thee from God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Refreshed and nourished with this spiritual food and drink we humbly beseech thee, O Lord, that by participation in this mystery thou wouldest teach us to despise earthly, and to love heavenly things. Through etc."}]},"adv2-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in the second week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 80","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Zech. viii. 3–8","inc":"Thus saith the Lord … in righteousness.","ref":"zechariah 8:3-8"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xi. 11–15","inc":"Verily I say … let him hear.","ref":"matthew 11:11-15"}]},"adv2-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday in the second week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 80","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Is. lxii. 6–12","inc":"I have set watchmen … not forsaken.","ref":"isaiah 62:6-12"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John i. 15–18","inc":"John bare witness … declared him.","ref":"john 1:15-18"}]},"adv3":{"title":"Mass of the Third Sunday in Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 80","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice. Let your moderation be known unto all men. The Lord is at hand. Be careful for nothing; but in everything by prayer and supplication let your requests be made known unto God.\nPs. And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Incline thine ear, we beseech thee, O Lord, to our prayers, and lighten the darkness of our minds by the grace of thy visitation. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"1 Cor. iv. 1–5","inc":"Let a man so account … praise of God,","ref":"1 corinthians 4:1-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou, O Lord, that sittest upon the cherubims, stir up thy strength and come.\n℣. Hear, O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep. Alleluya.\n℣. Stir up, O Lord, thy strength and come and help us."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Thou who dost each earthly throne\nRule by thy right hand alone,\nRaise up thy great power and shine,\nShew thy flock thy face divine.\nSaving gifts on him bestow\nWhom the prophets did foreshow.\nFrom the palace of the sky,\nJesu, to our land draw nigh."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xi. 2–10","inc":"Now when John … way before thee.","ref":"matthew 11:2-10"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Lord, thou art become gracious unto thy land: thou hast turned away the captivity of Jacob. Thou hast forgiven the offence of thy people.\n℣. Thou hast covered all their sins. Thou hast taken away all thy displeasure.\n℣. Shew us thy mercy, O Lord, and grant us thy salvation."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the sacrifice of our devotion may be continually offered unto thee, and that it may both fulfil the institution of thy sacred mystery, and wonderfully work in us thy salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Say to them that are of a fearful heart, Be strong, fear not; behold our God will come and save us."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We implore thy mercy, O Lord, that being cleansed from sin by this thy divine assistance, we may be found prepared for the approaching feast. Through etc."}]},"adv3-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in Ember Week (Advent)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 82","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Drop down ye heavens from above, and let the skies pour down righteousness; let the earth open, and bring forth a Saviour.\nPs. And let righteousness spring up together; I the Lord have created it."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee. Almighty God, that the approaching solemnity of our redemption may both afford us succour in this present life, and bestow on us abundantly the rewards of eternal happiness. Through the same etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. ii. 1–5","inc":"And it shall come to pass … light of the Lord our God.","ref":"isaiah 2:1-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Lift up your heads, O ye gates, and be ye lift up, ye everlasting doors; and the King of Glory shall come in.\n℣. Who shall ascend into the hill of the Lord? or who shall rise up in his holy place? Even he that hath clean hands and a pure heart."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Hasten we beseech thee, O Lord, and tarry not; and grant us the assistance of thy strength from above; that they who trust in thy goodness may be sustained by the consolations of thy coming. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. vii. 10–15","inc":"","ref":"isaiah 7:10-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The Lord is nigh unto all them that call upon him: yea, all such as call upon him faithfully."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke i. 26–38","inc":"The angel Gabriel … according to thy word.","ref":"luke 1:26-38"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Hail, Mary, full of grace; the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb.\n℣. Therefore also that holy thing which shall be born of thee shall be called the Son of God."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We present offerings, O Lord, befitting this healthgiving fast: grant that by these offices we may be prepared for the nativity of the eternal Bread. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Emmanuel."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Being filled, O Lord, with the gift of thy salvation, we humbly pray thee, that rejoicing in the taste thereof we may by it be effectually renewed. Through etc."}]},"adv3-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday in Ember Week (Advent)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 84","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Be thou nigh at hand, O Lord; for all thy commandments are true. As concerning thy testimonies I have known long since: that thou hast grounded them for ever.\nPs. Blessed are those that are undefiled in the way: and walk in the law of the Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Stir up, we beseech thee, O Lord, thy power and come, that they who do trust in thy lovingkindness may be speedily delivered from all adversity. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xi. 1–3","inc":"There shall come forth a rod … of his reins.","ref":"isaiah 11:1-3"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Shew us thy mercy, O Lord, and grant us thy salvation.\n℣. Lord, thou art become gracious unto thy land: thou hast turned away the captivity of Jacob."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke i. 39–47","inc":"And Mary arose … God my Saviour.","ref":"luke 1:39-47"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Wilt thou not turn again, and quicken us, O God, that thy"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that by the saving sacrifice we may be more readily prepared for those days, in which that mystery is to be celebrated, on which all the fulfilment of thy commands doth wait. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Behold, the Lord shall come, and all his saints with him; and there shall be a great light in that day."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Fulfil, O Lord, we beseech thee, thy mercy to thy people that prayeth; and grant that being refreshed with the abundance of thy gifts they may both more earnestly ask for a greater measure of thy grace, and also may more confidently hope to obtain the same. Through etc,"}]},"adv3-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday in Ember Week (Advent)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 85","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Come, O Lord, thou that sittest upon the cherubims; shew us the light of thy countenance, and we shall be whole.\nPs. Hear, O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who seest that we are afflicted through our own wickedness, mercifully grant that we may be consoled by thy visitation. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xix. 20–22","inc":"They shall cry … shall heal them.","ref":"isaiah 19:20-22"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"It goeth forth from the uttermost part of the heaven, and runneth about unto the end of it again.\n℣. The heavens declare the glory of God: and the firmament sheweth his handiwork."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that we who from long bondage are bowed down under the yoke of sin may by the expected new birth of thy Only-begotten Son be set free. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xxxv. 1–7","inc":"The wilderness … springs of water.","ref":"isaiah 35:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"In them hath he set a tabernacle for the sun: which cometh forth as a bridegroom out of his chamber."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, to us thy unworthy servants, that we who are saddened by the guilt of our own deeds may be gladdened by the advent of thy only-begotten Son. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xl. 9–11","inc":"O Zion … in his bosom.","ref":"isaiah 40:9-11"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Turn us again, O Lord God of hosts: shew the light of thy countenance, and we shall be whole.\n℣. Stir up thy strength, and come and help us."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the approaching solemnity of thy Son may both bring us healing in the present life, and win for us reward everlasting. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xlv. 1–8","inc":"Thus saith the Lord … have created it.","ref":"isaiah 45:1-8"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Stir up thy strength, O Lord, and come and help us.\n℣. O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep; shew thyself thou that sittest upon the Cherubims, before Ephraim, Benjamin and Manasses."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee, favourably to hear the prayers of thy people; that we who are justly punished for our offences may be comforted by the visitation of thy lovingkindness. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Clerks. ℣. Blessed art thou, O Lord God of our fathers."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who for the three children didst quench the flames of fire, mercifully grant that we thy servants may not be consumed by the flame of our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Thess. ii. 1–8","inc":"Now we beseech … of his coming.","ref":"2 thessalonians 2:1-8"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Hear, O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadeth Joseph like a sheep."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke iii. 1–6","inc":"Now in the fifteenth year … salvation of God.","ref":"luke 3:1-6"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion; shout, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold thy King cometh unto thee: he is holy, and having salvation."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Look down, we beseech thee, O Lord, with the eye of favour upon these offerings of the faithful; and because we have no strength of our own merits, may we rather by these gifts be made acceptable to thee at thy coming. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"He rejoiceth as a giant to run his course. It goeth forth from the uttermost part of the heaven, and runneth about unto the end of it again."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord our God, that these holy mysteries, which thou hast given unto us for our restoration and defence, may be made to us a source of healing both now and for evermore. Through etc."}]},"adv4":{"title":"Mass of the Fourth Sunday in Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 90","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Remember us, O Lord, according to the favour that thou bearest unto thy people; O visit us with thy salvation; that we may see the felicity of thy chosen; and rejoice in the gladness of thy people, and give thanks with thine inheritance.\nPs. We have sinned with our fathers, we have done amiss, and dealt wickedly."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, raise up, we pray thee, thy power and come, and with great might succour us, that whereas through our sins we are sore let and hindered, thy bountiful grace and mercy may speedily help and deliver us. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Phil. iv. 4–7","inc":"Rejoice in the Lord … in Christ Jesus.","ref":"philippians 4:4-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The Lord is nigh unto all them that call upon him: yea, all such as call upon him faithfully.\n℣. My mouth shall speak the praise of the Lord: and let all flesh give thanks unto his holy name. Alleluya."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Before the all-creating Lord\nLet us rejoice with one accord,\nWho made the worlds, the beaming sky,\nThe stars that glitter variously;\nThe sun, creation's central light.\nThe moon which softly decks the night,\nAll other orbs that gleam around.\nSea, land, hills, plains, and deeps profound;\nThe air, where fly the feather'd tribes,\nThe winds go forth, the tempest rides;\nAll, now and ever, thee alone.\nCeaselessly praising, Father own;\nWho to this lower earth hast sent\nThine only Son, all innocent.\nBringing salvation from on high,\nFor our transgressions here to die.\nTo thee, blest Trinity, we pray,\nGuide all our goings in thy way,\nControl our wills, our hearts revive,\nTo our offences pardon give."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John i. 19–28","inc":"The Jews sent … John was baptizing.","ref":"john 1:19-28"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Be strong, fear not; behold our God will come with vengeance, even God with a recompense; he will come and save us.\n℣. Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened, and the ears of the deaf shall be unstopped. Then shall the lame man leap as an hart, and the tongue of the dumb sing."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, look graciously upon this present sacrifice, and grant that by it we may be purified to take part in the nativity of thy Son, Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Emmanuel."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Accompany thy people, we beseech thee, O Lord, with the plenteousness of thy gifts, that they being defended from all evil by the virtue of thy sacraments, may be prepared both in mind and body to celebrate the ineffable mystery. Through etc."}]},"adv4-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in the fourth week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 92","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Joel ii. 23,24; iii. 17–21","inc":"Be glad then … dwelleth in Zion.","ref":"joel 2:23-24|joel 3:17-21"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke vii. 17–28","inc":"And this rumour … greater than he.","ref":"luke 7:17-28"}]},"adv4-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday in the fourth week of Advent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 92","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Zech. ii. 10–13","inc":"Sing and rejoice … holy habitation.","ref":"zechariah 2:10-13"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark viii. 15–26","inc":"And he charged … in the town.","ref":"mark 8:15-26"}]},"xmasEve":{"title":"Mass: Christmas Eve","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 93","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"To-day ye shall know that the Lord will come and save you, and in the morning, then shall ye see his glory.\nPs. The earth is the Lord's, and all that therein is: the compass of the world, and they that dwell therein."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who makest us glad with the yearly expectation of our redemption, grant that as we joyfully receive thy only begotten Son for our redeemer, so we may also with sure confidence behold him when he shall come to be our judge, even Jesus Christ, thy Son, our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lxii. 1–4","inc":"For Zion's sake … shall be married.","ref":"isaiah 62:1-4"},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. i. 1–6","inc":"Paul a servant … of Jesus Christ.","ref":"romans 1:1-6"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"To-day ye shall know that the Lord will come and save you, and in the morning, then shall ye see his glory.\n℣. Hear, O thou Shepherd of Israel, thou that leadest Joseph like a sheep. Shew thyself also thou that sittest upon the cherubims, before Ephraim, Benjamin, and Manasses."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. To-morrow the iniquity of the earth shall be blotted out, and the Saviour of the world shall reign over us."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Before the all-creating Lord etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt. i. i8–2i","inc":"When as his mother … from their sins.","ref":"matthew 1:18-21"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Lift up your heads, O ye gates, and be ye lift up, ye everlasting doors; and the King of glory shall come in."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant to us, we beseech thee, almighty God, that like as we do anticipate the adorable birth of thy Son, so we may receive with joy his everlasting gifts. Through the same etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The glory of the Lord shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see the salvation of our God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant to us, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we may be refreshed by the rehearsal of the birth of thy Only-begotten Son, whose heavenly mysteries form our food and drink. Through the same etc."}]},"xmas1":{"title":"Mass: Christmas Day: the Mass at Cock-crow","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 95","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The Lord hath said unto ce."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"0 God, who hast caused this most holy night to shine with the illumination of the true light; grant, we beseech thee, that as we have known the mysteries of this light on earth, we may also attain to the full enjoyment of it in heaven. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. ix. 2, 6, 7","inc":"The people that walked … even for ever.","ref":"isaiah 9:2|isaiah 9:6-7"},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Titus ii. 11–15","inc":"For the grace … speak and exhort.","ref":"titus 2:11-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"In the day of the power shall the people offer thee free-will offerings with an holy worship: the dew of thy birth is of the womb of the morning.\n℣. The Lord said unto my Lord: Sit thou on my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool. Alleluya."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"All hosts, above, beneath,\nSing the incarnate Lord,\nWith instruments and pious breath\nAttune each measured word.\nThis is the hallow'd morn\nWhen on our fallen race\nIn full effulgence rose the dawn\nOf new-born joy and grace.\nGlory to God on high,\nOn this renowned night\nWas thundered forth in harmony\nBy angel legions bright.\nAmazing splendours shone —\nA strange unwonted sight —\nUpon the shepherds biding lone\nUnder the veil of night.\nSudden, while peacefully\nThey watch'd their sheep-folds still.\nGood tidings wafted from on high\nTheir ears attentive fill.\nWho was before all time\nIs born of purest Maid;\nGlory to God in heights sublime,\nPeace comes the world to aid.\nE'en thus the choir on high\nSings praises jubilant,\nFrom pole to pole their voices fly,\nHeaven echoes to their chant.\nLet all with thrilling voice\nGive back the glorious lay,\nLet the wide universe rejoice,\nThat God is born this day.\nBurst are the iron chains\nWhich held the world in thrall;\nThe cruel foe no longer reigns,\nPeace is restored to all.\nFor lo! an order new\nDoth the glad world adorn;\nLet all things render praises due\nUnto the Virgin-born.\nHe all upholds alone,\nHe all alone did frame;\nMay he who hath such pity shown\nBlot out our sin and shame."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ii. 1–14","inc":"And it came to pass … toward men.","ref":"luke 2:1-14"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I/Ct the heavens rejoice, and let the earth be glad, for he Cometh."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the offering of this day's festival may be acceptable to thee, that by the favour of thy grace, through this holy communion, we may be found in the likeness of him, in whom our nature is united to thine, Jesus Christ our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The dew of thy birth is of the womb of the morning."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord our God, that we who celebrate with joy the birth of our Lord Jesus Christ, may by a worthy course of life be found meet to attain to fellowship with him. Who with thee etc."}]},"xmas2":{"title":"Mass: Christmas Day: the Mass at Daybreak","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 100","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Light shall shine upon us to-day, because unto us the Lord is born, and he shall be called Wonderful, God the Prince of Peace, the everlasting Father, of whose kingdom there shall be no end.\nPs. The Lord is King, and hath put on glorious apparel: the Lord hath put on his apparel, and girded himself with strength."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee. Almighty God, unto us on whom is largely shed the new light of thy incarnate Word, that as by faith it enlightens the mind, so also it may shine forth in action. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lxi. 1–3 ; IxII. 11, 12","inc":"The spirit of the Lord … not forsaken.","ref":"isaiah 61:1-3|isaiah 62:11-12"},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Titus iii. 4–7","inc":"But after that … eternal life.","ref":"titus 3:4-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord. God is the Lord who hath shewed us light.\n℣. This is the Lord's doing: and it is marvellous in our eyes. Alleluya.\n℣. The Lord is King and hath put on glorious apparel: the Lord hath put on his apparel, and girded himself with strength."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Unto the King new-born new praises sing,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ii. 15–20","inc":"The shepherds said … told unto them.","ref":"luke 2:15-20"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"He hath made the round world so sure that it cannot be moved."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the offerings which we bring may be suitable to the mysteries of this day's nativity; that like as he who was born as man shone forth as God, so this earthly substance may impart to us that which is divine. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion: shout, O daughter of Jerusalem, behold thy King cometh unto thee, holy, and the Saviour of the world."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we may be evermore restored by the sacramental and commemorative ' Usia. Ps. xciii. 2, 3. 3 Salvator mundi; adapted from Zech. ix. 9. renewal of the marvellous birth of him whose nativity cast oiF the old man. Through etc."}]},"xmas3":{"title":"Mass: Christmas Day: the Third Mass","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 103","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Unto us a Child is born, unto us a Son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder, and his name shall be called the angel of great counsel.\nPs. O sing unto the Lord a new song, for he hath done marvellous things."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who are held in bondage by the old yoke of sin may be set free by the new birth in the flesh of thy only-begotten Son. Through the same etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lii. 6–10","inc":"Therefore my people … of our God.","ref":"isaiah 52:6-10"},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Heb. i. 1–12","inc":"God who at sundry … shall not fail.","ref":"hebrews 1:1-12"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"All the ends of the world have seen the salvation of our God: shew yourselves joyful unto the Lord, all ye lands.\n℣. The Lord declared his salvation: his righteousness hath he openly shewed in the sight of the heathen. Alleluya.\n℣. The hallowed day hath shone upon us: come ye nations, and adore the Lord, for to-day a great light hath descended on the earth."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"This day celestial melody\nResounded o'er the earth,\nWhat time the virgin bare a Son,\nSeraphs proclaimed his birth.\nWhat aileth thee, thou world below?\nHaste thee with them to sing;\nIn pastoral charge the shepherds watch,\nHark! angel voices ring,\nChanting their strains of holy joy.\nWith glory fraught and peace.\nTo Christ they render homage due,\nTo us they sing of grace.\nNot unto all such gifts are given,\nBut to the good of heart.\nNot irrespectively bestowed,\nBut measured by desert.\nAffections must be weaned from sin,\nSo shall we gain that peace within\nReserved for pure in heart;\nLo! earth is join'd with things divine,\nIn this respect their lays combine.\nBut fitly fall apart.\nO man, rejoice, and ponder this accord;\nO flesh, rejoice, associate with the Word.\nThis verse shall be repeated thrice.\nHis rising by the stars is told\nWith indicating light;\nLo! star-lit chiefs to Bethlehem\nFollow that planet bright.\nThe King of heaven is cradled found\nAmid the beasts he made.\nIn The\na rude manger's\nLord of all is narrow\nlaid. bed\n' Source unknown.\nStar of the sea! thy blessed Son\nThe holy Church adores.\nThat he our service will accept\nDevoutly she implores."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John i. 1–14","inc":"In the beginning … grace and truth.","ref":"john 1:1-14"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The heavens are thine, the earth also is thine: thou hast laid the foundation of the round world, and all that therein is. Righteousness and equity are the habitation of thy seat."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Sanctify, O Lord, the gifts we offer by the new Birth of thy only-begotten Son; and mercifully cleanse us from the stains of our sins. Through the same etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"All the ends of the world have seen the salvation of our God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that as the Saviour of the world, who was born to-day, bestoweth upon us a heavenly birth, so he may also himself bestow on us the gift of immortality. Who with thee etc."}]},"12/26":{"title":"Mass of St. Stephen, Protomartyr","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 105","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Princes also did sit and speak against me, and wicked men did persecute me. Help me, O Lord, my God, for thy servant is occupied in thy statutes!\nPs. Blessed are those that are undefiled in the way: and walk in the law of the Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant to us, Lord, we beseech thee, to imitate that which we commemorate, that we may learn to love even our enemies, forasmuch as we celebrate the feast of him who knew how to pray even for his persecutors to our Lord Jesus Christ, thy Son. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts vi. 8–10 ; vii. 54–60","inc":"Stephen, full of faith … fell asleep.","ref":"acts 6:8-10|acts 7:54-60"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Princes also did sit and speak against me; and wicked men did persecute me."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Great is the Lord in all the earth,"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Princes also did sit and speak against me: and wicked men have persecuted me.\n℣. Help me, O Lord my God: O save me according to thy mercy. Alleluya."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxiii. 34–39","inc":"Wherefore, behold I send … name of the Lord.","ref":"matthew 23:34-39"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Apostles chose Stephen, a Levite, a man full of faith and of the Holy Ghost; whom the Jews stoned, praying and saying. Lord Jesus, receive my spirit. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O Lord, we beseech thee, these gifts in commemoration of the holy martyr Stephen, and grant that as his passion made him glorious, so our devotion may make us blameless. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"I see the heavens opened, and Jesus standing on the right hand of the power of God. Lord Jesus, receive my spirit: and lay not this sin to their charge, for they know not what they do."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the mysteries which we have received, O Lord, be our assistance, and by the intercession of thy blessed protomartyr Stephen, evermore strengthen and protect us. Through etc."}]},"12/27":{"title":"Mass of St. John the Evangelist","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 108","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"In the midst of the congregation he opened his mouth; and the Lord filled him with the spirit of wisdom and understanding, and clothed him with the robe of glory."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Merciful Lord, we beseech thee to cast thy bright beams of light upon thy church, that it being enlightened by the doctrine of thy blessed Apostle and Evangelist saint John may attain to the gift of everlasting life. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Ecclus. xv. 1–6","inc":"He that feareth … everlasting name.","ref":"sirach 15:1-6"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Then went this saying abroad among the brethren, that that disciple should not die,\n℣. But, if I will that he tarry till I come, what is that to thee? Alleluya."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"O John! disciple chaste, whom Jesus lov'd,\nThou for the love which thou didst bear to him\nDidst leave thy earthly parent in the ship;\nUnwedded didst Messiah follow, drinking\nPure streams of wisdom from his holy breast.\nThou saw'st the glory of the Son of God\nWhilst yet alive on earth, whom to behold\nThe saints do look only in life eternal.\nTo thee, Christ, triumphing upon the cross,\nIn charge his [holy] Mother did commit,\nThat to the virgin thou, thyself a virgin,\nWith filial care should'st minister protection.\nIn prison cast, and torn with cruel scourges,\nThou didst rejoice to bear Christ's testimony.\nThou, too, didst raise the dead, and deadly poison\nDidst drink, and take no harm, in Jesu's name.\nTo thee the most high Father doth reveal\nHis word of prophecy, denied to others.\nDo thou, O John, belov'd of Jesus Christ,\nCommend us all to God, in never-ceasing prayers!"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John xxi. 19–24","inc":"Follow me … testimony is true.","ref":"john 21:19-24"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The righteous shall flourish like a palm-tree: and shall spread abroad like a cedar in Libanus."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O Lord, we beseech thee, the gifts which we offer to thee on his feast by whose patronage we trust to be delivered. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Then went this saying abroad among the brethren, that that disciple should not die: yet Jesus said not unto him, He shall not die; but, If I will that he tarry till I come, what is that to thee?"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We humbly beseech thee, O Lord, that having been refreshed with heavenly food and drink, we may be fortified by the prayers of him on whose commemoration day we have received them. Through etc."}]},"12/28":{"title":"Mass of Holy Innocents","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 110","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Out of the mouth of very babes and sucklings, O God, hast thou ordained strength, because of thine enemies.\nPs. O Lord our Governor, how excellent is thy Name in all the world!"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, whose praise the martyred Innocents shewed forth this day not by their speech but by their death, mortify all evil vices in us, that our life may shew forth by its actions that faith which we profess with our lips. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rev. xiv. 1–5","inc":"And I looked … throne of God.","ref":"revelation 14:1-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Our soul is escaped even as a bird out of the snare of the fowler."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let children sing high melodies,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. ii. 13–18","inc":"The angel of the Lord … they are not.","ref":"matthew 2:13-18"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Our soul is escaped even as a bird out of the snare of the fowler: the snare is broken and we are delivered."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Be present, O Lord, at the consecration of these gifts on the feast of the Innocents, and grant that as we venerate their infancy dedicated to thee, so we may be able to imitate their guilelessness. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"In Rama was there a voice heard, lamentation and great mourning, Rachel weeping for her children, and would not be comforted, because they are not."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, O Lord, that by the prayers of the holy Innocents, the votive offerings of which we have partaken may both support us in our present life, and bring us to the life eternal. Through etc."}]},"12/29":{"title":"Mass of St. Thomas the Martyr (Becket)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 112","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let us all rejoice in the Lord, and celebrate a feast in honour of Thomas the martyr; over whose passion the angels rejoice, and praise the Son of God.\nPs. Hear my voice, O God, in my prayer: preserve my life from fear of the enemy."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, in behalf of whose Church thy glorious bishop Thomas fell by the swords of wicked men; grant, we beseech thee, that all who implore his help may effectually obtain their petitions. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Heb. v. 1–4, 6","inc":"Every high priest … order of Melchisedech.","ref":"hebrews 5:1-4|hebrews 5:6"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou shalt set, O Lord, etc, Alleluya.\n℣. Thou shalt crown him etc."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"This day let solemn strains\nResound on earth below.\nAnd o'er the martyr's palm\nTriumph the heavenly host.\nWhat do ye, joyous folk?\nGive thanks with them above.\nLet every living soul rejoice,\nAnd with free voice to Christ sing praise.\nLet Canterbury at this feast\nDevoutly homage pay.\nThe furious soldier band\nShouts forth\nLawless the tyrant\nwill and king's command,\nfierce decree\nForced their way full haughtily.\nArmed men with passion wild\nPlaces dear to Christ defiled:\nBut Christ's footsteps following,\nThomas with unswerving tread\nStood unshaken, undismayed.\nIn obedience to his King\nMeets the sword with steady eye,\nCounting it all gain to die.\nThomas, rejoice, thy victory adds a lay\nTo swell the praise of Christ's own natal day.\nThe martyr's glory is proclaimed,\nBy divers signs assured,\nWithin the fane the pastor chief\nA cruel death endured;\nNor day nor place from murderous hand\nAwe or respect procured,\nStar of the sea, who didst rejoice to feed\nChrist at thy holy breast,\nHim do we humbly pray, that in the end we may\nWith Thomas surely rest,\nAnd through his prayer be blest."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xix. 12–28","inc":"A certain nobleman … up to Jerusalem.","ref":"luke 19:12-28"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Thou shalt set, O Lord, etc."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that blessed Thomas, Bishop and Martyr, may obtain for us, that the gift of the saving offering now to be consecrated may avail to our salvation; so that we may both know the excellency of his conversation, and partake of the benefit of his intercession. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"His honour is great etc"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Almighty and merciful God, grant unto us, through these holy gifts which we have received, that the revered intercession of thy blessed Martyr and Bishop may assist us, who for the honour of thy name was deemed worthy to be crowned with a glorious martyrdom. Through etc."}]},"xmas6":{"title":"Mass: The Sixth Day from Christmas","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 115","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"For while all things were in quiet silence, and that night was in the midst of her swift course, thine Almighty \"Word, O Lord, leaped down from heaven out of thy royal throne.\nPs. The Lord is King, and hath put on glorious apparel: the Lord hath put on his apparel, and girded himself with strength."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, direct our actions according to thy good pleasure, that in the name of thy dear Son we may be found worthy to abound in good works. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Gal. iv. 1–7","inc":"The heir as long as he is … heir through God.","ref":"galatians 4:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou art fairer than the children of men: full of grace are thy lips."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let us celebrate this day, Christ the Lord's nativity;"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ii. 33–40","inc":"And Joseph and … was upon him.","ref":"luke 2:33-40"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"God hath made the round world so sure that it cannot be moved. Ever since the world began hath thy seat been prepared: thou art from everlasting."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Accept, we beseech thee, O Lord, the sacrifice ot thy people, and direct that they may have fellowship with Jesus Christ thy Son our Lord who deigned to be a partaker of our humanity. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Take the young child and his mother, and go into the land of Judah: for they are dead which sought the young child's life."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the sacrifice having been now received, thy whole Church may rejoice in him who hath taken upon himself her infirmities, that she might become herself a partaker of the divine substance. Through etc."}]},"12/31":{"title":"Mass of St. Silvester","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 117","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let thy priest"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that the revered feast of the blessed Silvester, thy confessor and bishop, may both increase our devotion, and further our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Behold, a high priest... sweet savour. Alleluya. '} 1 have found David etc."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":""},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxv. 14–23","inc":"A certain man travelling … joy of thy Lord.","ref":"matthew 25:14-23"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I have found David etc."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Be present, we beseech thee, O Lord, at our oblations, and suffer not those who are fortified by the glorious confession of blessed Silvester, thy confessor and bishop, to be exposed to any perils of mind or body. Through etc."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, Almighty God, that through the intercession of blessed Silvester, thy confessor and bishop, the gift which we have received at this day's feast may impart salvation both to our bodies and souls. Through etc."}]},"1/1":{"title":"Mass of the Circumcision of our Lord","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 118","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"is born etct"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who permittest unto us to celebrate the octave of our Saviour's birth, grant, we beseech thee, that as we have been renewed by the communion of his flesh, so we may be defended by his perpetual Divinity. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Titus ii. 11–15","inc":"For the grace of God … speak and exhort.","ref":"titus 2:11-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"All the ends etc Alleluya.\n℣. God, who at sundry times and in divers manners spake in time past unto the fathers by the prophets, hath in these last days spoken unto us by his Son."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let us devoutly pay\nWith joy and praises meet,\nOur reverence to this holy day.\nWhich dawns with radiance sweet.\nDarkness hath passed away,\nThe mist of night retires,\nThe day star of the sea to-day\nWith health the world inspires.\nHe of her womb is born\nBefore whom hell doth shake,\nAnd conscious of his empire shorn\nGrim death himself doth quake.\nDespoiled the serpent mourns.\nWho wrought the world's annoy;\nFall'n man, the erring sheep, returns.\nRestored to endless joy.\nWith songs the angelic host\nMake all the heaven resound.\nThat the tenth piece, which once was lost,\nThis day is safely found.\nO offspring highly blest,\nRedeeming man forlorn,\nGod, maker of the world confest,\nIs of a woman born.\nWhat marvel passing strange\nThis nature doth enfold I\nTaking the new without a change,\nRetaining still the old.\nThe very Godhead is\nIn human flesh arrayed:\nWhat ear of earthly witnesses\nHath heard such things essayed?\nTo seek that which was lost\nThe shepherd good came down;\nLike warrior armed at his post\nA helm his head doth crown.\nOn his own darts the foe\nHimself doth headlong thrust;\nStripped of his arms he lieth low.\nWherein he put his trust.\nDivided is the spoil,\nThe captor captive ta'en;\nChrist's valiant fight the foe doth foil,\nAnd sure salvation gain.\nThen to his home on high\nOur champion led the way.\nTriumphing in his victory,\nWhere thou art praised for aye."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ii. 21","inc":"And when eight days … in the womb.","ref":"luke 2:21"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The heavens are thine etc"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that by these gifts which we bring in honour of the mystery of the hidden birth of our Lord Jesus Christ, we may attain to the understanding of a cleansed soul. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"All the ends etc"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that this which we have received on the recurring commemoration of our Saviour's birth may procure for us the healing of salvation. Through etc."}]},"epiEve":{"title":"Mass: Vigil of the Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 121","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Light shall shine etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the brightness of the coming festival may illuminate our hearts, that so we may be able to free ourselves from the darkness of this world, and may reach the land of eternal light. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Titus iii. 4–7","inc":"But after that the kindness … eternal life.","ref":"titus 3:4-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Blessed is he that cometh etc.\n℣. This is the Lord's doing etc."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"is always said on this day, whether it be a Sunday or not.\n℣. The Lord is King etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. ii. 19–23","inc":"But when Herod was dead … a Nazarene,","ref":"matthew 2:19-23"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"He hath made the round world so sure etc."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that in this present sacrifice we may both offer and receive him whom the ' As on p. 113. = Ps. cxviii. 26, 27. jj,_ j. devout gifts of the coming solemnity proclaim beforehand, Jesus Christ thy Son our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Take the young child etc."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant to us. Lord, we beseech thee, worthily to celebrate the mystery, which in the infancy of our Saviour is declared by brilliant miracles, while his humanity is manifestly shewn by his bodily growth. Through etc."}]},"1/6":{"title":"Mass: The Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 124","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Behold the Lord, the ruler, cometh; and dominion power and empire are in his hand.\nPs. Give the king thy judgments, O God: and thy righteousness unto the king's son."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who by the leading of a star didst to-day manifest thy only begotten Son to the gentiles; mercifully grant that we who know thee now by faith may be admitted to the vision of thy majesty. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lx. 1–6","inc":"Arise, shine … of the Lord.","ref":"isaiah 60:1-6"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"All they from Sheba shall come: they shall bring gold and incense; and they shall shew forth the praises of the Lord.\n℣. Arise and shine, O Jerusalem, for the glory of the Lord is risen upon thee. Alleluya.\n℣. We have seen his star in the east, and are come with gifts to adore the Lord."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let us duly magnify\nThis renowned Epiphany,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. ii. 1–12","inc":"When Jesus was born … another way.","ref":"matthew 2:1-12"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The kings of Tharsis and of the isles shall give presents, the kings of Arabia and Saba bring gifts. All kings shall fall down before him: all nations shall do him service."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Regard, we beseech thee, O Lord, with favour the gifts of thy Church, whereby are now no longer offered gold, frankincense, and myrrh, but what is signified by the same gifts is sacrificed and received, Jesus Christ our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"We have seen his star in the east, and are come with gifts to adore the Lord."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that what we celebrate in this solemn office we may apprehend with the intelligence of a purified mind. Through etc."}]},"epiSun":{"title":"Mass: Sunday within the Octave of the Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 126","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John i. 29–34","inc":"The next day John … is the Son of God.","ref":"john 1:29-34"}]},"1/13":{"title":"Mass: Octave of the Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 127","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Behold the Lord, the ruler, cometh etc"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, whose only-begotten Son appeared in the substance of our flesh, grant, we beseech thee, that we may be found worthy to be inwardly renewed by him, whom we have known to resemble ourselves in outward appearance. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xxv. 1; xli. 18; lii. 13; xii. 3–5 (put together)","inc":"O Lord, thou art my God … in all the earth.","ref":"isaiah 25:1|isaiah 41:18|isaiah 52:13|isaiah 12:3-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"All they from Sheba etc"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"IT\", We have seen his star etc"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. iii. 13–17","inc":"Then cometh Jesus … I am well pleased.","ref":"matthew 3:13-17"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The kings of Tharsis etc."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We bring thee offerings, O Lord, in honour of the manifestation of the birth of thy Son; humbly beseeching thee, that as the same our Lord Jesus Christ is the author of our gifts, so he may also himself mercifully receive the same. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"We have seen his star etc."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy heavenly light may always and everywhere prevent us; that we may contemplate with clear vision, and receive with due effect the mystery of which thou hast been pleased that we should be partakers. Through etc."}]},"epi1":{"title":"Mass of the First Sunday after the Octave of Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 129","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"I saw seated on a high throne, a man whom a multitude of angels worshipped, chanting together; behold him, the name of whose kingdom is for everlasting.\nPs. O be joyful in the Lord all ye lands; serve the Lord with gladness."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee, mercifully to receive the prayers of thy people which call upon thee, that they may both perceive what things they ought to do, and also may have power to fulfil the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xii. 1–5","inc":"I beseech you therefore … one of another.","ref":"romans 12:1-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Blessed be the Lord God, the God of Israel; thou who alone from eternity doest great and wonderful things.\n℣. The mountains also shall bring peace, and the little hills righteousness unto the people."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. O joyful i\" the Lord, all ye lands; serve the Lord with gladness."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ii. 42–52","inc":"And when he was twelve … God and man.","ref":"luke 2:42-52"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O be joyful in the Lord all ye lands; be joyful in the Lord all ye lands: serve the Lord with gladness, and come before his presence with a song. Be ye sure that the Lord he is God."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the sacrifice offered unto thee may ever quicken and defend us. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Son, why hast thou thus dealt with us? Behold, thy father and I have sought thee sorrowing. And how is it that ye sought me? Wist ye not that I must be about my Father's business?"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"In partaking, O Lord, of thy holy gifts may we both receive the effect of perfect purification, and the continual help of divine protection. Through etc."}]},"epi1-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 131","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. x. 1–4","inc":"Brethren, my heart's desire … that believeth.","ref":"romans 10:1-4"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. iv. 12–17","inc":"Now when Jesus had heard … is at hand.","ref":"matthew 4:12-17"}]},"epi1-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 131","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xiii. 1–6","inc":"Let every soul … this very thing.","ref":"romans 13:1-6"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke iv. 14–22","inc":"And Jesus returned … out of his mouth.","ref":"luke 4:14-22"}]},"epi2":{"title":"Mass of the Second Sunday after the Octave of Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 131","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let all the world worship thee, thee and praise thy name, O most highest.\nPs. O be joyful in God all ye lands: sing praises unto the honour of his name, make his praise to be glorious."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, who dost govern all things in heaven and earth; mercifully hear the supplications of thy people, and grant us thy peace all the days of our life. Through etc."},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The Lord sent his word and healed them: and they were saved from their destruction."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. Praise him all ye angels of his: praise him all his host."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John ii. 1–11","inc":"There was a marriage … believed on him.","ref":"john 2:1-11"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O be joyful in God all ye lands; be joyful in God all ye lands: sing praises unto the honour of his name. O come hither and hearken, all ye that fear God, and I will tell you what things the Lord hath done for my soul."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to cleanse thy people from all contamination of frowardness, that their offerings may be acceptable unto thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord saith, Fill the water pots with water, and bear unto the governor of the feast. And when the ruler of the feast had tasted the water that was made wine, he saith unto the bridegroom, Thou hast kept the good wine until now. This was the first miracle that Jesus did in the presence of his disciples."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that the efficacy of thy power may be increased in us, that being quickened by divine sacraments, we may through thy gifts be made ready evermore to receive what they promise. Through etc."}]},"epi2-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 133","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Tim. i. 15–17","inc":"This is a faithful saying … Amen.","ref":"1 timothy 1:15-17"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark vi. 1–6","inc":"And he went out … their unbelief","ref":"mark 6:1-6"}]},"epi2-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 133","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xiv. 14–23","inc":"I know and am persuaded … is sin.","ref":"romans 14:14-23"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke iv. 31–37","inc":"And he came down … country round about.","ref":"luke 4:31-37"}]},"epi3":{"title":"Mass of the Third Sunday after the Octave of Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 133","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Worship him, all ye gods. Sion heard of it, and rejoiced:and the daughters of Judah were glad.\nPs. The Lord is King, the earth may be glad thereof: yea, the multitude of the isles may be glad thereof."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, mercifully look upon our infirmities, and stretch forth the right hand of thy majesty to defend us. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xii. 16–21","inc":"Be not wise in … evil with good.","ref":"romans 12:16-21"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The heathen shall fear thy name, O Lord: and all the kings of the earth thy majesty."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. The Lord is King, the earth may be glad thereof: yea, the multitude of the isles may be glad thereof."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. viii. 1–13","inc":"When he was come down … selfsame hour.","ref":"matthew 8:1-13"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The right hand of the Lord hath the pre-eminence: the right hand of the Lord bringeth mighty things to pass. I shall not die, but live: and declare the works of the Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We celebrate the adorable mysteries of eternal life, beseeching thee that by devout sacrifices and by good works we may attain the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"They all wondered at the words which proceeded out of the mouth of God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we to whom thou vouchsafest the use of so great mysteries, may be made truly worthy to receive the effects thereof. Through etc."}]},"epi3-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 134","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xv. 30–33","inc":"Now I beseech you … Amen.","ref":"romans 15:30-33"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark iii. 1–5","inc":"And he entered … as the other.","ref":"mark 3:1-5"}]},"epi3-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 134","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. iii. 16–23","inc":"Know ye not … Christ is God's.","ref":"1 corinthians 3:16-23"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. iv. 23–25","inc":"And Jesus went … multitudes of people.","ref":"matthew 4:23-25"}]},"epi4":{"title":"Mass of the Fourth Sunday after the Octave of Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 134","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Worship him etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who knowest us to be set in the midst of so many and great dangers that by reason of the frailty of our nature we cannot stand upright, grant to us such health of mind and body, that by thy aid we may overcome those things which we suffer for our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. xiii. 8–10","inc":"Owe no man … fulfilling of the law.","ref":"romans 13:8-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The heathen shall fear etc"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. viii. 23–27","inc":"And when he was entered … obey him.","ref":"matthew 8:23-27"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The right hand etc"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that the offering of this sacrifice may cleanse and defend our frailty from all evil. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"They all wondered etc"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May thy gifts, O God, both detach us from earthly delights, and ever strengthen us with heavenly food. Through etc."}]},"epi4-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 135","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. vii. 1–5","inc":"It is good for a man … your incontinency.","ref":"1 corinthians 7:1-5"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke ix. 57–62","inc":"And it came to pass … kingdom of God.","ref":"luke 9:57-62"}]},"epi4-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 136","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. vii. 20–24","inc":"Let every man … abide with God.","ref":"1 corinthians 7:20-24"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark x. 13–16","inc":"And they brought … and blessed them.","ref":"mark 10:13-16"}]},"epi5":{"title":"Mass of the Fifth Sunday after the Octave of Epiphany","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 136","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Worship him etc"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee to keep thy houshold in continual godliness, that they who do lean only upon the hope of thy heavenly grace, may ever be defended by thy protecting power. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Col. iii. 12–17","inc":"Put on, therefore … Father by him.","ref":"colossians 3:12-17"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The heathen shall fear etc"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xiii. 24–30","inc":"","ref":"matthew 13:24-30"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The right hand etc"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the offerings and prayers of thy servants, that by the help of thy protection, they may not lose what they have obtained, and may get those things which they desire. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"They all wondered etc."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"O God who dost approach us in our participation of thy sacrament, work out in our hearts the effects of its power, that through the gift itself we may be made fit for the divine gift which we have received. Through etc."}]},"epi5-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 137","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Tim. ii. 1–7","inc":"I exhort therefore … in faith and verity.","ref":"1 timothy 2:1-7"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxi. 28–32 and xiii. 9","inc":"A certain man … let him hear.","ref":"matthew 21:28-32|matthew 13:9"}]},"sept":{"title":"Mass: Septuagesima Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 137","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The sorrows of death compassed me: the pains of hell came about me. In my trouble I will call upon the Lord, so shall he hear my voice out of his holy temple.\nPs. I will love thee, O Lord my strength; the Lord is my strong rock and my defence, and my Saviour."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee favourably to hear the prayers of thy people, that we who are justly punished for our offences may be mercifully delivered for the glory of thy name. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor, ix. 24–27 ; x. 1–4","inc":"Know ye not … Rock was Christ.","ref":"1 corinthians 9:24-27|1 corinthians 10:1-4"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"A refuge in due time of trouble. And they that know thy Name will put their trust in thee; for thou, Lord, hast never failed them that seek thee.\n℣. For the poor shall not alway be forgotten: the patient abiding of the meek shall not perish for ever. Up, Lord, and let not man have the upper hand."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Out of the deep have I called unto thee, O Lord: Lord, hear my voice."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xx. 1–16","inc":"The kingdom of heaven … few chosen.","ref":"matthew 20:1-16"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"It is a good thing to give thanks unto the Lord: and to sing praises unto thy Name, O Most Highest."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Having received, O Lord, we beseech thee, our gifts and prayers, both cleanse us by these heavenly mysteries, and mercifully hear us. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Shew thy servant the light of thy countenance, and save me for thy mercy's sake. Let me not be confounded, O Lord,*« lb. lb. s,I for 2, have called upon thee."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May thy faithful people, O Lord, be strengthened by thy gifts, that both by receiving them they may seek them anew, and by seeking them may receive them evermore. Through etc."}]},"sept-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 139","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Cor. iv. 3–12","inc":"If our gospel be hid … life in you.","ref":"2 corinthians 4:3-12"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark ix. 30–37, and iv. 23","inc":"Jesus departed thence … let him hear.","ref":"mark 9:30-37|mark 4:23"}]},"sept-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 139","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Cor. iv. 13–18","inc":"We having the same … are eternal.","ref":"2 corinthians 4:13-18"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xii. 30–37","inc":"He that is not with me … be condemned.","ref":"matthew 12:30-37"}]},"sexa":{"title":"Mass: Sexagesima Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 140","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Up, Lord, why sleepest thou? Awake and be not absent from us for ever. Wherefore hidest thou thy face, and forgettest our misery and trouble? Our belly cleaveth unto the ground. Arise, Lord, help us and deliver us.\nPs. We have heard with our ears, O God, our fathers have told us."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God who seest that we put not our trust in anything that we do, mercifully grant that by the protection of the teacher of the gentiles we may be defended against all adversities. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"1 Cor. xi. 19–33 ; xii. 1–9","inc":"Ye suffer fools … rest upon me.","ref":"1 corinthians 11:19-33|1 corinthians 12:1-9"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Let the nations know that thy name is Lord, and that thou only art the Most Highest over all the earth."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Thou hast moved the land, O Lord, and divided it."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke viii. 4–15","inc":"When much people … with patience.","ref":"luke 8:4-15"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"0 hold thou up my goings in thy paths, that my footsteps slip not. Incline thine ear unto me, and hearken unto my words. Shew thy marvellous lovingkindness, thou that art the Saviour of them which put their trust in thee.\n℣. Hear the right, O Lord, consider my complaint, and hearken unto my prayer."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Regard, we beseech thee, O Lord, the offering of thy family, and grant that those whom thou makest to be partakers of thy holy gifts, may attain unto the fulness of the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"1 will go unto the altar of God, even unto the God of my joy and gladness."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Almighty God, we humbly beseech thee to grant that those whom thou refreshest with thy sacrament may worthily serve thee in a life well pleasing unto thee. Through etc."}]},"sexa-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 141","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Cor. i. 23, 24; ii. 1–11","inc":"I call God … of his devices.","ref":"2 corinthians 1:23-24|2 corinthians 2:1-11"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark iv. 1–9","inc":"Jesus began to teach … let him hear.","ref":"mark 4:1-9"}]},"sexa-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 141","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Cor. v. 11–15","inc":"Knowing the terror of … and rose again.","ref":"2 corinthians 5:11-15"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xvii. 20–37","inc":"When he was demanded … gathered together.","ref":"luke 17:20-37"}]},"quin":{"title":"Mass: Quinquagesima Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 142","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Be thou my strong rock, and house of defence, that thou mayest save me. For thou art my strong rock and my castle: be thou also my guide and lead me for thy Name's sake.\nPs. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust; let me never be put to confusion, deliver me in thy righteousness, and save me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee, mercifully hear our prayers, and loose us from the chains of our sins, and keep us from all adversity. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. xiii. 1–13","inc":"Though I speak … is charity.","ref":"1 corinthians 13:1-13"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou art the God that doest wonders, and hast declared thy power among the people."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O be joyful in the Lord all ye lands; serve the Lord with gladness.\n℣. Come before his presence with a song.\n℣. Be ye sure that the Lord he is God."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xviii. 31–43","inc":"Then he took … praise unto God.","ref":"luke 18:31-43"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Blessed art thou, O Lord, O teach me thy statutes. Blessed art thou, O Lord, O teach me thy statutes. With my lips have I been telling of all the judgments of thy mouth.\n℣. Blessed are those that are undefiled in the way, and walk in the law of the Lord. Blessed are they that keep his testimonies, and seek him with their whole heart.\n℣. O turn from thy people shame and rebuke, because we have not forgotten thy law, O Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that this offering may cleanse away our sins; and sanctify the bodies and souls of thy servants for the celebration of this sacrifice. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"They did eat, and were well filled; for the Lord gave them their own desire; they were not disappointed of their lust."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, almighty God, that we who have received heavenly food, may be thereby defended against all adversities. Through etc."}]},"ash":{"title":"Mass: Ash Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 143","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Thou hast mercy upon all, O Lord, and hatest nothing that thou hast made; and winkest at the sins of men, because they should amend. But thou sparest all, because thou art the Lord our God. ' Partly from Joel ii. 17. = Partly from Wisdom xi. 23, 24, 26.\nPs. Be merciful unto me, O Lord, be merciful unto me, for my soul trusteth in thee."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, unto thy faithful people, that they may both enter upon the holy solemnities of the fast with befitting piety, and pass through them with undisturbed devotion. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Joel ii. 12–19","inc":"Therefore also now … among the heathen.","ref":"joel 2:12-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Be merciful unto me, O God, be merciful unto me, for my soul trusteth in thee.\n℣. He shall send from heaven; and save me from the reproof of him that would eat me up."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. vi. i6–21","inc":"Moreover, when ye fast … heart be also.","ref":"matthew 6:16-21"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I will magnify thee, O Lord, for thou hast set me up: and not made my foes to triumph over me. O Lord my God, I cried unto thee, and thou hast healed me. a JJ. Thou, Lord, hast brought my soul out of hell; thou hast kept my soul from them that go down into the pit."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Make us, we beseech thee, O Lord, to be duly fitted to present these offerings, by which we celebrate the beginning of this venerable fast. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"In the law of the Lord will he exercise himself day and night:"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the sacrament which we have received, O Lord, be our support, that our fast may both be acceptable to thee, and also avail to our healing. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"over the people. Look mercifully, O Lord, upon those who bow themselves down before thy majesty, that they who have been refreshed with the divine gift, may ever be nourished by help from heaven. Through etc."}]},"ash-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after Ash Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 151","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"When I cried unto the Lord, he heard my voice from the battle that was against me; and he that endureth for ever shall bring them down. O cast thy burden upon the Lord, and he shall nourish thee.\nPs. Hear my prayer, O God, and hide not thyself from my petition. Take heed unto me, and hear me. Glory be to the Father etc. When I cried etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who art offended by sin and propitiated by repentance, mercifully regard the prayers of thy people which call upon thee, and graciously turn away the scourge of thine anger, which we deserve for our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xxxviii. 1–6","inc":"In those days … defend this city.","ref":"isaiah 38:1-6"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"O cast thy burden upon the Lord, and he shall nourish thee.\n℣. When I cried unto the Lord, he heard my voice from the battle that was against me."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. viii. 5–13","inc":"And when Jesus … selfsame hour.","ref":"matthew 8:5-13"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul; my God, I have put my trust in thee: O let me not be confounded, neither let mine enemies triumph over me. For all they that hope in thee shall not be ashamed.\n℣. Lead me forth in thy truth and learn me; for thou art the God of my salvation; in thee hath been my hope all the day long."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We offer to thee, O Lord, the gifts which thou hast given, that they may both help our mortal nature which thou hast created, and work in us an immortal cure. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Then shalt thou be pleased with thy sacrifice of righteousness, with the burntofferings and oblations upon thine altar, O Lord."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant we beseech thee, O Lord, to thy faithful people, that they may both continually receive the paschal sacraments, and earnestly look forward to that which is approaching; so that stedfastly abiding in the mysteries by which they have been renewed, they may by this means be brought to newness of life. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Spare, O Lord, spare thy people, that having been justly chastised, they may be revived by thy mercy. Through etc."}]},"ash-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after Ash Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 153","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Hear, O Lord, and have mercy upon me: Lord, be thou my helper.\nPs. I will magnify thee, O Lord, for thou hast set me up, and hast not made my foes to triumph over me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Graciously favour us, O Lord, we beseech thee, in the fast which we have begun, that we may be enabled to fulfil with pure minds the duties which we outwardly perform. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. Iviii. 1–9","inc":"Cry aloud … Here I am.","ref":"isaiah 58:1-9"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"One thing have I desired of the Lord, which I will require, even that I may dwell in the house of the Lord.\n℣. That I may see the will of the Lord, and be protected by his holy temple."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. v. 43–48, and vi. 1–6","inc":"Ye have heard … shall reward thee openly.","ref":"matthew 5:43-48|matthew 6:1-6"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Quicken me, O Lord, according to thy word, that I may know thy testimonies."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We offer, O Lord, the sacrifice of Lenten observance; grant, we beseech thee, that it may both render our minds acceptable unto thee, and dispose us more readily to continence. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Serve the Lord in fear, and rejoice unto him with reverence; kiss the Son lest ye perish from the right way."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that the heavenly gifts which we ofttimes approach with due devotion may prove continually profitable to our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Guard thy people, O Lord, and graciously cleanse them from all their sins; for no adversity shall harm them, if no wickedness get the dominion over them. Through etc."}]},"ash-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after Ash Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 154","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Hear me, O Lord, and have mercy upon me: Lord, be thou my helper.\nPs. I will magnify thee, O Lord, for thou hast set me up: and not made my foes to triumph over me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Hearken, O Lord, to our supplications, that we may observe with devout service this holy fast which was instituted to give health and salvation to our souls and bodies. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. Iviii. 9–14","inc":"If thou take away … hath spoken it.","ref":"isaiah 58:9-14"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"One thing have I desired of the Lord which I will require, even that I may dwell in the house of the Lord."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark vi. 47–56","inc":"When even was come … were made whole.","ref":"mark 6:47-56"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Quicken me, O I-ord, according to thy word, that I may know thy testimonies.\n℣. Give me understanding that I may know thy law, and let the free-will offerings of my mouth please thee, O Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Prepare us, O Lord, we beseech thee, for the bounden duties of this fast, that we may ever celebrate this adorable sacrifice with sober minds. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Serve the Lord with fear, and rejoice unto him with reverence. Kiss the Son, lest ye perish from the right way."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee. Almighty God, that invigorated with the gift of heavenly life, we may find that what to us is a mystery in this present life may prove to be a help to life eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Make us, we beseech thee, O Lord, both at all times to frequent with devout observance the means of our salvation, and with still greater devotion to attend them after their special commencement. Through etc."}]},"q1":{"title":"Mass of the First Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 156","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"He shall call upon me, and I will hear him. I will deliver him, and bring him to honour. \"With long life will I satisfy him.\nPs. Whoso dwelleth under the defence of the most High shall abide under the shadow of the Almighty."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who purifiest thy church by the annual observation of Lent, grant unto thy family that what they endeavour to obtain of thee by fasting they may follow up by good works. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"2 Cor. vi. 1–10","inc":"We beseech you … possessing all things.","ref":"2 corinthians 6:1-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"He shall give his angels charge over thee to keep thee in all thy ways.\n℣. They shall bear thee in their hands, that thou hurt not thy foot against a stone."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"FIRST SUNDAY IN LENT. M"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. iv. 1–11","inc":"Then was Jesus led up … ministered unto him.","ref":"matthew 4:1-11"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"He shall defend thee under his wings, and thou shalt be safe under his feathers: his faithfulness and truth shall be thy shield and buckler."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Solemnly we offer our sacrifice at the beginning of Lent, beseeching thee, O Lord, that as we abstain from carnal feastings, so likewise we may be withheld from hurtful pleasures. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"He shall defend thee under his wings, and thou shalt be safe under his feathers: his faithfulness and truth shall be thy shield and buckler."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May, the holy drink-offering of thy sacrament, O Lord, create us anew and through laying aside the old man may it make us partakers of this saving mystery. Through etc."}]},"q1-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after the First Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 158","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Behold, even as the eyes of servants look unto the hand of their masters: even so our eyes wait upon the Lord our God, until he have mercy upon us. Have mercy upon us, O Lord, have mercy upon us.\nPs. Unto thee lift I up mine eyes, O thou that dwellest in the heavens."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Turn thou us, O God our Saviour, and that the Lenten fast may benefit us, instruct our minds with heavenly teaching. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Ezek. xxxiv. 11–16","inc":"Thus saith the Lord … with judgment.","ref":"ezekiel 34:11-16"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Behold, O Lord our defender, and look upon thy servants.\n℣. O Lord God of hosts, hear the prayers of thy servants."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"0 Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxv. 31–46","inc":"When the Son of man … life eternal.","ref":"matthew 25:31-46"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"1 will lift up mine eyes, that I may see the wondrous things of thy law; that thou mayest teach me thy righteousness. O grant me understanding that I may know thy testimonies.\n℣. Lay thy law upon me, O Lord, and let me learn the way of thy righteousness, and I will be exercised in thy precepts."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the offerings of our devotion may be acceptable unto thee; that through thy operation they may both sanctify our fast, and obtain for us the favour of thy comfort. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"I did call upon the Lord with my voice: and he heard me out of his holy hill. I will not be afraid for ten thousands of the people: that have set themselves against me round about."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the frequenting of thy mysteries may aid us, and both wean us from earthly desires, and train us to love heavenly things. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Loose us, we beseech thee, O Lord, from the chains of our sins, and mercifully turn away from us the punishment which we deserve for them. Through etc."}]},"q1-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after the First Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 160","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Lord, thou hast been our refuge from one generation to another. Thou art God from everlasting, and world without end.\nPs. Before the mountains were brought forth, or ever the earth and the world were made."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Look down, O Lord, upon this thy family, and grant that while we chastise ourselves by mortifying the flesh, our minds may be inflamed with desire of thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lv. 6–11","inc":"Seek ye the Lord … whereto I sent it.","ref":"isaiah 55:6-11"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Let my prayer be set forth in thy sight as the incense, O Lord.\n℣. Let the lifting up of my hands be an evening sacrifice."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxi. 10–17","inc":"When he was come … into Bethany.","ref":"matthew 21:10-17"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"My hope hath been in thee, O Lord: I have said. Thou art my God. My time is in thy hand.\n℣. Shew thy servant the light of thy countenance, and save me for thy mercy's sake, O Lord: let me not be confounded, for I have called upon thee."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that our devotion which we offer unto thee may be in accordance with the gift which we have dedicated to thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Hear me when I call, O God of my righteousness: thou hast set me at liberty when I was in trouble; have mercy upon me, O Lord, and hearken unto my prayer."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Almighty God, we beseech thee that we may effectually obtain that salvation, of which we have received the pledge in these mysteries. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"May our prayers ascend to thee, O Lord, and keep far all iniquity from thy church. Through etc."}]},"q1-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in Ember Week in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 161","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Call to remembrance, O Lord, thy tender mercies, and thy lovingkindnesses which have been ever of old. Neither let our enemies triumph over us. Deliver us, O God of Israel, out of all our troubles.\nPs. Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul: my God, I have put my trust in thee: O let me not be confounded."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Mercifully hear our prayers, O Lord, we beseech thee; and stretch forth the right hand of thy majesty to be our defence against all our enemies. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Exod. xxiv. I2–18","inc":"The Lord said … and forty nights.","ref":"exodus 24:12-18"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The sorrows of my heart are enlarged: O bring thou me out of my troubles, O Lord.\n℣. Look upon my adversity and misery: and forgive me all my sin."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Regard graciously, O Lord, we beseech thee the devotion of thy people, that they who are mortified in the body by abstinence, may be renewed in mind by the fruit of good works. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"i Kings xix. 3–8","inc":"He arose and went … the mount of God.","ref":"1 kings 19:3-8"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xii. 38–50","inc":"Then certain … and sister and mother.","ref":"matthew 12:38-50"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"My delight shall be in thy commandments which I have loved. My hands also will I lift up unto thy commandments which I have loved."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Look we beseech thee, O Lord, upon this our only sacrifice; that we, who have waited for these things which we believe and hope for in the reception of these mysteries, may obtain the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Consider my meditation. O hearken thou unto the voice of my calling, my king, and my God: for unto thee will I make my prayer."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that these holy gifts may cleanse away our guilt, and work in us the fruit of good living. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to illuminate our minds with the bright beams of thy light, that we may both perceive what things we ought to do, and also may have power rightly to fulfil the same. Through etc."}]},"q1-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after the First Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 163","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Glory and worship are before him: power and honour are in his sanctuary.\nPs. O sing unto the Lord a new song: sing unto the Lord, all the whole earth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, who hast appointed the observances of fasting and almsgiving as remedies against sin, grant to us ever to be devoted to thee both in mind and body. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Ezek. xviii. 1–19","inc":"The word of the Lord … shall surely live.","ref":"ezekiel 18:1-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Keep me as the apple of an eye: hide me under the shadow of thy wings.\n℣. Let my sentence come forth from thy presence: and let thine eyes look upon the thing that is equal."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John viii. 31–47","inc":"Then said Jesus … heareth God's words.","ref":"john 8:31-47"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Angel of the Lord tarrieth round about them that fear him. O taste and see how gracious the Lord is.\n℣. I will alway give thanks unto the Lord: his praise shall ever be in my mouth."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that those sacrifices which have been instituted to accompany this wholesome fast may favourably conduce to our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The bread which I will give is my flesh, which I will give for the life of the world."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"By the free grant of these thy gifts, O Lord, increase our safety in things temporal, and renew it in things eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, to all Christian people that they may acknowledge what they profess, and love the heavenly gift which they often approach. Through etc."}]},"q1-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday in Ember Week in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 165","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Bring thou me out of my troubles, O Lord. Look upon my adversity and misery: and forgive me all my sin.\nPs. Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul; my God, I have put my trust in thee. Let me not be confounded."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Be favourable, O Lord, to thy people, and pitifully comfort again with thy gracious help those whom thou makest to be devout towards thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Ezek. xviii. 20–28","inc":"The soul that sinneth … shall not die.","ref":"ezekiel 18:20-28"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"My God, save thy servant that putteth his trust in thee.\n℣. Give ear. Lord, unto my prayer."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John v. 1–15","inc":"After this there was … made him whole.","ref":"john 5:1-15"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Bless the Lord, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits; making thee young and lusty as an eagle.\n℣. Who forgiveth all thy sin, and saveth thy life from destruction: who crowneth thee with mercy and lovingkindness."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O Lord, we beseech thee, the offerings of our homage, and graciously sanctify thine own gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"All mine enemies shall be confounded and sore vexed: they shall be turned back, and put to shame suddenly."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, O Lord, that by the operation of this mystery our sins may both be washed away, and our just desires fulfilled. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Hear us, O merciful God, and shew the light of thy grace within our minds. Through etc."}]},"q1-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday in Ember Week in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 166","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let my prayer enter into thy presence, incline thine ear unto my calling, O Lord.\nPs. O Lord God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before thee."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, look graciously upon thy people, and mercifully turn away from them the scourge of thine anger. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Deut. xxvi. 15–19","inc":"Look down from thy … as he hath spoken.","ref":"deuteronomy 26:15-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Be merciful, O Lord, unto our sins. Wherefore do the heathen say. Where is now their God?\n℣. Help us, O Lord God of our salvation, and for the glory of thy name deliver us, O Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who leadest us through things temporal to things eternal, extend thy mercy to us who are striving after heavenly promises; and because it is wholly thy gift that we believe in thee, may it also be thy gift that we live in thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Deut. xi. 22–25","inc":"If ye shall diligently … said unto you.","ref":"deuteronomy 11:22-25"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Behold, O God our defender, and look upon the face of thy servants."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Behold, O Lord our protector, and grant that we who are pressed down by the weight of our own evils, may receive thy pity, and serve thee with a quiet mind. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"2 Mace. i. 23, 2–5","inc":"The priest made … time of trouble.","ref":"2 maccabees 1:23|2 maccabees 1:2-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Turn thee again, O Lord, at the last, and be gracious unto thy servants.\n℣. Lord, thou hast been our refuge from one generation to another."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Hearken, we beseech thee, O Lord, unto our prayers, that through thy bountiful grace, we may be both humble in prosperity and safe in adversity. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Ecclus. xxxvi. 1–8","inc":"Have mercy … thy wonderful works.","ref":"sirach 36:1-8"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"O Lord, save thy people, and bless thine heritage."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Prevent us, O Lord, we beseech thee, in all our doings, and further us with thy continual help; that all our works may be begun, continued, and ended in thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Song of the Three Children 24–32.","inc":"The angel … above all for ever.","ref":"daniel 3:49-50|daniel 3:47-48|daniel 3:51-58|daniel 3:61-66|daniel 3:71|daniel 3:67-68|daniel 3:74-76|daniel 3:79-87"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Blessed art thou in the firmament of heaven: and to be praised and exalted above all for ever."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who for the three children didst quench the flames of fire, mercifully grant that we thy servants may not be consumed by the flame of our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Thess. v. 14–23","inc":"Now we exhort … our Lord Jesus Christ.","ref":"1 thessalonians 5:14-23"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O praise the Lord, all ye heathen: praise him all ye nations."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xvii. 1–9","inc":"After six days … again from the dead.","ref":"matthew 17:1-9"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Sanctify, O Lord, we beseech thee, our fast by this present sacrifice, that what we outwardly profess by its observance, may be inwardly wrought in us. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O Lord my God, in thee have I put my trust: save me from all them that persecute me, and deliver me."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Defend, O Lord, with thy perpetual protection those whom thou feedest with the divine mysteries; and guide with thy saving consolations those whom thou hast filled with heavenly ordinances. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O Lord, let thy longed-for blessing strengthen thy faithful people, both making them never to swerve from thy will, and bestowing on them the continual joy of thy favour. Through etc."}]},"q2":{"title":"Mass of the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 171","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Call to remembrance, O Lord, thy tender mercies: and thy lovingkindnesses which have been ever of old. Let"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty God, who seest that we have no power of ourselves to help ourselves, keep us both outwardly in our bodies, and inwardly in our souls; that we may be defended from all adversities which may happen to the body, and from all evil thoughts which may assault and hurt the soul. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Thess. iv. 1–7","inc":"We beseech … but unto holiness.","ref":"1 thessalonians 4:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The sorrows of my heart are enlarged: O bring thou me out of my troubles, O Lord.\n℣. Look upon my adversity and misery: and forgive me ail my sin. Tract."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xv. 21–28","inc":"Then Jesus went … from that very hour.","ref":"matthew 15:21-28"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"And my delight shall be in thy commandments, which I have loved: my hands also will I lift up unto thy commandments, which I have loved."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Graciously receive, O Lord, the gifts of thy church, which thou in thy mercy hast ordained to be offered, and which in thy power thou causest to pass into the mystery of our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Consider my meditation; hearken thou unto the voice of my calling my King and my God; for unto thee will I make my prayer, O Lord."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the grace of the most sacred body and blood which we have received may quicken us, and that it may largely bestow in everlasting effect that which it doth promise in these mystic actions. Through etc."}]},"q2-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 173","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Deliver me, O Lord, and be merciful unto me: my foot standeth right, I will praise the Lord in the congregations.\nPs. Be thou my judge, O Lord, for I have walked innocently;my trust hath been also in the Lord, therefore shall I not fall."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that thy family, which mortify their flesh by abstinence from meat, may likewise fast from sin, and follow righteousness. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Dan. ix. 15–19","inc":"And now, O Lord our God … by thy name.","ref":"daniel 9:15-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou art my helper and my redeemer, O Lord: make no long tarrying.\n℣. Let mine enemies be ashamed and confounded that seek"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"after my soul,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John viii. 2i–29","inc":"Then said Jesus … that please him.","ref":"john 8:21-29"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I will thank the Lord for giving me warning. I have set God always before me; for he is on my right hand, therefore I shall not fall.\n℣. Preserve me O God, for in thee have I put my trust. O my soul, thou hast said unto the Lord, thou art my God. The Lord himself is the portion of mine inheritance."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Let this sacrifice of propitiation and praise, O Lord, make us worthy of thy reconciliation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O Lord our Governor, how excellent is thy name in all the world."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May this communion, O Lord, cleanse us from sin, and make us partakers of the heavenly remedy. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Hearken unto our supplications, almighty God, and mercifully grant that they on whom thou bestowest boldness to hope for thy pity may experience the effect of thy wonted lovingkindness. Through etc."}]},"q2-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 175","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"My heart hath talked of thee, seek ye my face: thy face, Lord, will I seek. O hide not thou thy face from me.\nPs. The Lord is my light and my salvation: whom then shall I fear?"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Graciously perfect in us, O Lord, we beseech thee, the help of this holy observance, that what through thy guidance we have known to be our duty, we may through thy working indeed fulfil. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"i Kings xvii. 8–16","inc":"The word of the Lord … spake by Elijah.","ref":"1 kings 17:8-16"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"0 cast thy burden upon the Lord, and he shall nourish thee.\n℣. As for me I will call upon God, and he shall hear my voice from the battle that was against me."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxiii. 1–12","inc":"Then spake Jesus … shall be exalted.","ref":"matthew 23:1-12"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Have mercy upon me, O Lord, after thy great goodness: do away, O Lord, mine offences.\n℣. For I acknowledge my faults: and my sin is ever before me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Be pleased, O Lord, to work out in us thy sanctification by these mysteries: that it may both cleanse us from earthly vices, and lead us onward to receive heavenly gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"1 will speak of all thy marvellous works. I will be glad and rejoice in thee: yea, my songs will I make of thy name, O thou most Highest."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that what we have received at thy holy altar, may be the medicine both of soul and body; that we who are fortified by the participation of so great a remedy, may not be wearied out by any adversities. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O Lord, graciously accept our prayers, and heal the infirmities of our souls, that having received the remission of our sins, we may ever rejoice in thy blessing. Through etc."}]},"q2-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 176","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Forsake me not, O Lord my God; be not thou far from me. Haste thee to help me, O Lord God of my salvation.\nPs. Put me not to rebuke, O Lord, in thine anger, neither chasten me in thy displeasure."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee graciously to behold thy people, and grant that those whom thou commandest to abstain from fleshly food, may also cease from hurtful vices. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Esther xiii. 11–17","inc":"Thou art Lord … praise thee, O Lord.","ref":"esther 13:11-17"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"O Lord, save thy people, and give thy blessing unto thine inheritance. iJ\", Unto thee will I cry, O Lord my strength: think no scorn of me, lest I become like them that go down into the pit."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Bring thou me out of my troubles etc"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xx. 17–28","inc":"And Jesus going up … ransom for many.","ref":"matthew 20:17-28"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Unto thee, O Lord, will I lift up my soul; my God, I have put my trust in thee; O let me not be confounded, neither let my enemies triumph over me; for all they that hope in thee shall not be ashamed."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Graciously look, O Lord, upon the offerings which we make unto thee, and by this most sacred intercourse absolve us from the bonds of our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The righteous Lord loveth righteousness: his countenance will behold the thing that is just."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the frequent reception of these mysteries may be profitable to us; so that being cleansed from the condition of the old man, we may progress and increase in newness of life. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O God, the restorer and lover of innocence, draw to thyself the hearts of thy servants, that being inflamed with the fervour of thy spirit, they may be found steadfast in faith, and active in good works. Through etc."}]},"q2-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 178","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Haste thee, O Lord, to deliver me: make haste to help me, O Lord. Let mine enemies be ashamed and confounded that seek after my soul.\nPs. Let them be turned backward and put to confusion that wish me evil."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant unto us we beseech thee, O Lord, the help of thy grace, that, duly giving ourselves up to fasting and prayer, we may be delivered from the enemies both of our souls and bodies. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jer. xvii. 5–10","inc":"Cursed is the man … fruit of his doings.","ref":"jeremiah 17:5-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Be merciful to our sins, O Lord. Wherefore do the heathen say. Where is now their God?"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John v. 30–47","inc":"I can of mine own self … believe my words.","ref":"john 5:30-47"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Moses prayed before the Lord his God and said; Moses prayed before the Lord his God and said; Lord, why doth thy wrath wax hot against thy people? Turn from thy fierce wrath; remember Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; to whom thou swearest to give a land flowing with milk and honey. And the Lord repented of the evil which he thought to do unto his people.\n℣. The Lord said unto Moses, Thou hast found grace in my sight, and I know thee before all men. And Moses made haste, and bowed himself to the ground, and worshipped saying; I know that thou keepest mercy for thousands, forgiving iniquity and sin."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"O God, to whom we now offer a single offering, the variety of all earlier sacrifices having passed away, hearken unto the prayers which have been conceived under thy inspiration, and bid the desires of them that hope in thee to be fulfilled, and their sins to be blotted out. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"He that eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood, dwelleth in me, and I in him, saith the Lord."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy grace may never leave us, but that it may make us intent on thy holy service, and ever procure for us thy abundant help, and defend us from all adversities. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Be favourable, O Lord, to thy servants, and bestow thy perpetual lovingkindness on them that ask thee; that they who glory in thee as their creator and governor may have all blessings restored and perpetuated to them. Through etc."}]},"q2-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 179","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"But as for me, I will behold thy presence in righteousness: and when I awake up after thy likeness, shall be satisfied with it.\nPs. Hear the right, O Lord, and consider my complaint."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that thou wouldest make us who have been purified with this holy fast, to come to the approaching feast with pure minds. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Gen. xxxvii. 6–22","inc":"Here I pray you … his father again.","ref":"genesis 37:6-22"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"When I was in trouble, I called upon the Lord, and he heard me.\n℣. Deliver my soul, O Lord, from lying lips: and from a deceitful tongue."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxi. 33–46","inc":"There was a certain householder … for a prophet.","ref":"matthew 21:33-46"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Make haste, O Lord, to help me. Let them be ashamed and confounded together, that seek after my soul to destroy it.\n℣. Let them be driven backward, and put to rebuke that wish me evil."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May these sacrifices, O Lord God, both actively abide with us, and by their effectual working be confirmed. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Thou shalt keep them, O Lord: thou shalt preserve him from this generation for ever."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Make us, O Lord, we beseech thee, who have received the pledge of eternal salvation, so to direct our course agreeably thereto, that we may be able to attain unto the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant unto thy people, we beseech thee, O Lord, health both of mind and body; that they, cleaving to good works, may ever be worthy to be defended by thy mighty protection. Through etc."}]},"q2-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after the Second Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 181","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The law of the Lord is an undefiled law, converting the soul: the testimony of the Lord is sure, and giveth wisdom unto the simple.\nPs. The heavens declare the glory of God: and the firmament sheweth his handy-work."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, a saving effect to our fasts, that the chastisement of our flesh which we have undertaken may redound to the quickening of our souls. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Gen. xxvii. 6–39","inc":"And Rebekah spake … heaven from above.","ref":"genesis 27:6-39"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"It is a good thing to give thanks unto the Lord: and to sing praises unto thy name, O most Highest.\n℣. To tell of thy lovingkindness early in the morning: and of thy truth in the night season."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xv. 11–32","inc":"A certain man … lost and is found.","ref":"luke 15:11-32"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Lighten mine eyes that I sleep not in death, lest mine enemy say, I have prevailed against him.\n℣. How long wilt thou forget me, O Lord, for ever? How long shall I seek counsel in my soul?"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Mercifully grant, O Lord, by this holy sacrifice, that we who pray to be freed from our own sins may not be burdened by the sins of others. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Son, it was meet that thou shouldest be glad, for this thy brother was dead and is alive again; and was lost, and is found."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the taste of thy divine sacrament, O Lord, penetrate the innermost recesses of our hearts, and make us plentifully to partake of its effects. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Keep, we beseech thee, O Lord, this thy family with thy continual goodness; that they who do lean only on the hope of thy heavenly grace, may also be defended by thy heavenly power. Through etc."}]},"q3":{"title":"Mass of the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 182","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Mine eyes are ever looking unto the Lord: for he shall pluck my feet out of the net. Turn thee unto me"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, Almighty God, look upon the hearty desires of thy humble servants, and stretch forth the right hand of thy majesty to be our defence. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Eph. v. 1–9","inc":"Be ye therefore followers … righteousness and truth.","ref":"ephesians 5:1-9"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Up, Lord, and let not man have the upper hand: let the heathen be judged in thy sight.\n℣. While mine enemies are driven back: they shall fall and perish at thy presence."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Unto thee lift I up mine eyes, O thou that dwellest in the heavens."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xi. 14–28","inc":"Jesus was casting out … and keep it.","ref":"luke 11:14-28"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The statutes of the Lord are right and rejoice the heart: sweeter also than honey and the honey-comb. Moreover, by them is thy servant taught."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the gifts of thy devout servants; and of thy lovingkindness cleanse them who serve the divine mysteries, by which thou dost also justify them that are in ignorance. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Yea, the sparrow, hath found her an house, and the swallow a nest where she may lay her young: even thy altar, O Lord of hosts, my King and my God. Blessed are they that dwell in thy house, they will be always praising thee."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Mercifully deliver us, O Lord, from all guilt and danger, whom thou admittest to be partakers of this so great mystery. Through etc."}]},"q3-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 184","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"In God's word will I rejoice: in the Lord's word will I comfort me. Yea, in God have I put my trust: I will not fear what man can do unto me.\nPs. Be merciful unto me, O God, for man goeth about to devour me: he is daily fighting, and troubling me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee, mercifully to pour thy grace into our hearts, that like as we abstain from fleshly food, so also we may restrain our senses from hurtful excesses. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"2 Kings v. 1–15","inc":"Now Naaman … but in Israel.","ref":"2 kings 5:1-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou tellest my Sittings: put my tears into thy bottle.\n℣. Be merciful unto me, O Lord, for man goeth about to devour me: he is daily fighting and troubling me."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke iv. 23–30","inc":"Whatsoever we have heard … went his way.","ref":"luke 4:23-30"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Hear my prayer, O God: and hide not thyself from my petition. Take heed unto me, and hear me:\n℣. How I am vexed. The enemy crieth so and the ungodly Cometh on so fast. I would make haste to escape."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, O Lord, that the offering which we make unto thee of our bounden duty, may be made to us a sacrament profitable to our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Who shall give salvation unto Israel out of Sion? When the Lord turneth the captivity of his people: then shall Jacob rejoice, and Israel shall be glad."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant we beseech thee, almighty and merciful God, that what we touch with our lips we may receive with a pure mind. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Let thy mercy, O Lord, we beseech thee, assist us, that we may be accounted worthy to be rescued from the threatening dangers of our sins by thy protection, and to be saved by thy deliverance. Through etc."}]},"q3-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 186","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"I have called upon thee, O God, for thou shalt hear me: incline thine ear unto me, and hearken unto my words. Keep me as the apple of an eye: hide me under the shadow of thy wings.\nPs. Hear the right, O Lord, consider my complaint."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Hear us, almighty and merciful God, and mercifully grant unto us the gift of saving continence. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"2 Kings iv. 1–7","inc":"Now there cried … of the rest.","ref":"2 kings 4:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Cleanse me, O Lord, from my secret faults. Keep thy servant also from presumptuous sins."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt, xviii. 15–22","inc":"Moreover, if thy brother … seventy times seven.","ref":"matthew 18:15-22"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The right hand of the Lord hath the pre-eminence: the right hand of the Lord bringeth mighty things to pass. I shall not die but live: and declare the works of the Lord.\n℣. I called upon the Lord in my trouble: and he heard me at large. The Lord is on my side."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that our redemption may be made effectual through this sacrament; that it may withdraw us ever from worldly excesses, and lead us to all things profitable to our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Lord who shall dwell in thy tabernacle: or who shall rest upon thy holy hill? Even he that leadeth an uncorrupt life, and doeth the thing which is right."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that being cleansed by these holy mysteries, we may obtain both pardon and grace. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Defend us, O Lord, by thy protection, and ever keep us from all iniquity. Through etc."}]},"q3-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 187","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"My trust hath been in the Lord. I will be glad, and rejoice in thy mercy: for thou hast considered my trouble.\nPs. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust: let me never be put to confusion, deliver me in thy righteousness."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant unto us, we beseech thee, O Lord, that being disciplined by this wholesome fast, and abstaining from hurtful vices, we may the more readily obtain thy mercy. Through etc. P. 35. Ps. XV. I, 2. Ps. xxxi. 7, 8. lb. I."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Exod. xx. 12–24","inc":"Honour thy father … I will bless thee.","ref":"exodus 20:12-24"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Have mercy upon me, O Lord, for I am weak. O Lord heal me.\n℣. For my bones are vexed. My soul also is sore troubled."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Bring thou me out of my troubles etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt. xv. 1–20","inc":"Then came to Jesus … defileth not a man.","ref":"matthew 15:1-20"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"But deal thou with me, O Lord God, according unto thy name: for sweet is thy mercy.\n℣. Hold not thy tongue, O God of my praise: for the mouth of the deceitful is opened upon me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the prayers of thy people together with the offerings of their oblations; and defend us, who celebrate thy mysteries, from all dangers. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Thou shalt shew me the path of life: in thy presence is the fulness of joy, O Lord."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the holy table from which we have been fed sanctify us, we beseech thee, O Lord, so that being freed from all errors we may be made worthy to attain thy heavenly promises. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that we, who seek the grace of thy protection, may be delivered from all evils, and serve thee with a quiet mind. Through etc"}]},"q3-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 189","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"I am the salvation of the people, saith the Lord: in whatsoever distress they shall call upon me I will hear them, and I will be their Lord for ever.\nPs. Hear my law, O my people, incline your ears unto the words of my mouth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the holy devotion of this fast, may both make us pure, and render us acceptable to thy majesty. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jer. vii. 1–7","inc":"The word that came … for ever and ever.","ref":"jeremiah 7:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The eyes of all wait upon thee, O Lord: and thou givest them their meat in due season.\n℣. Thou openest thine hand: and fillest all things living with plenteousness."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vi. 27–35","inc":"Labour not for … shall never thirst.","ref":"john 6:27-35"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Though I walk in the midst of trouble, yet shalt thou refresh me, O Lord: thou shalt stretch forth thy hand upon the furiousness of mine enemies, and thy right hand shall save me.\n℣. When I called upon the Lord, thou heardest me: and enduedst my soul with much strength. ' Source unknown. Ps. Ixxviii. i. -pi. cxlv. 15."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee to make us approach thy holy mysteries with pure minds, that we may ever offer unto thee a suitable service. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Thou hast charged: that we shall diligently keep thy commandments. O that my ways were made so direct: that I might keep thy statutes!"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the reverent receiving of thy sacrament, O Lord, both cleanse us by its mystic working, and defend us by its abiding virtue. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy heavenly favour may increase thy people that is under thee, and make them always to keep thy commandments. Through etc."}]},"q3-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 190","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Shew some token upon me for good, that they who hate me may see it, and be ashamed: because thou Lord hast holpen me and comforted me.\nPs. Bow down thine ear, O Lord, and hear me: for I am poor, and in misery."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy gracious favour may accompany our fast, that as we abstain from food in our P. 35. Ps. cxix. 4, 5. Ps. ixxxvi. 17. t lb. i. bodies, so we may fast from sin in our minds. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Num. xx. 6–13.","inc":"In those days … sanctified in them.","ref":"numbers 20:6-13"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"My heart hath trusted in God, and I am helped: therefore my heart danceth for joy, and in my song will I praise him.\n℣. Unto thee will I cry, O Lord my strength, think no scorn of me, depart not from me."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John iv. 5–42","inc":"Then cometh he … the Saviour of the world.","ref":"john 4:5-42"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O hearken thou unto the voice of my calling, my King and my God: for unto thee will I make my prayer, O Lord.\n℣. Ponder my words, O Lord, consider my meditation, and hear me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Regard, O Lord, with thy favour the offerings which we consecrate, that they may both be acceptable to thee, and always further our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him, saith the Lord to the woman of Samaria, it shall be in him a well of water, springing up into everlasting life."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the receiving of this sacrament, O Lord, cleanse us from sin, and lead us to the heavenly kingdom. Through etc. With variation and additions. Ps. xxviii. 8."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that we who do trust in thy protection may by thy aid overcome all things that oppose us. Through etc"}]},"q3-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after the Third Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 192","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Ponder my words, O Lord: consider my meditation. hearken thou unto the voice of my calling.\nPs. My King and my God."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that they who for the mortification of the flesh abstain from food, may by following after righteousness fast from sin. Through etc."},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, will fear no evil: for thou art with me, O Lord.\n℣. Thy rod and thy staff comfort me."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John viii. 1–11","inc":"Jesus went unto … sin no more.","ref":"john 8:1-11"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Order my steps, O Lord, in thy word: and so shall no wickedness have dominion over me. 5r. When thy word goeth forth: it giveth light and understanding unto the simple."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May this offering, O Lord, we beseech thee, be made pleasing unto thee through our solemn fast; and that it may be the more acceptable, may it be presented unto thee from pure hearts. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Woman, hath no man condemned thee? Neither do I condemn thee: sin no more."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, almighty God, that we may be reckoned among his members, of whose body and blood we have received communion, Jesus Christ, thy Son, our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Stretch forth, O Lord, over thy faithful people the right hand of thy heavenly help, that they may seek thee with their whole heart, and that what they ask worthily they may obtain effectually. Through etc."}]},"q4":{"title":"Mass of the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 193","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Rejoice, O Jerusalem, and gather together, all ye that love the Lord: rejoice with joy for her, all ye that mourn for her, that ye may rejoice and be satisfied with the breasts of her consolation."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who for our evil deeds do worthily deserve to be punished, by the comfort of thy grace may be relieved. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Gal. iv. 22–31, v. 1","inc":"It is written … made us free.","ref":"galatians 4:22-31|galatians 4:1"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"I was glad when they said unto me: we will go into the house of the Lord.\n℣. Peace be within thy walls: and plenteousness within thy"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"palaces."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vi. 1–14","inc":"Jesus went over … into the world.","ref":"john 6:1-14"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O praise the Lord, for the Lord is gracious: O sing praises unto his name, for it is lovely."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant to us. Lord, we beseech thee, ever to be occupied In divine service, and alike in mind and body to be made meet for these holy mysteries. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Jerusalem is built as a city: that is at unity in itself. For"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, merciful God, that we may both handle with true reverence, and ever receive with faithful mind thy holy gifts with which we are fed unceasingly. Through etc."}]},"q4-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 195","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Save me, O God, for thy Name's sake: and avenge me in thy strength. Hear my prayer, O God.\nPs. For strangers are risen up against me, and tyrants seek after my soul."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who yearly celebrate these holy observances, may be wellpleasing to thee both in body and mind. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"i Kings iii. 16–28","inc":"Then came there two women … to do judgment.","ref":"1 kings 3:16-28"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"And be thou my strong rock, and house of my defence: that thou mayest save me.\n℣. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust: let me never be put to confusion."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John ii. 13–25","inc":"And the Jews' passover … what was in man.","ref":"john 2:13-25"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O be joyful in the Lord, all ye lands: serve the Lord with gladness, and come before his presence with a song. Be ye sure that the Lord he is God.\n℣. It is he that hath made us and not we ourselves; we are his people, and the sheep of his pasture."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Work In us, O Lord, we beseech thee, by thy mysteries, that we may offer these gifts to thee with worthy feelings. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O cleanse thou me from my secret faults. Keep thy servant also from presumptuous sins."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, sustain with thine aid those whom thou refreshest with thy divine sacraments, that those whom thou cherishest by temporal may be fed by eternal blessings. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Graciously hear our prayer, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we to whom thou givest a hearty desire to pray may obtain the help of thy defence. Through etc."}]},"q4-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 196","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Hear my prayer, O God: and hide not thyself from my petition. Take heed unto me, and hear me.\nPs. How I mourn in my prayer, and am vexed. The enemy crieth so."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the sacred fast which we observe may be for our increase in holy conversation, and may draw down upon us the constant succour of thy mercy. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Exod. xxxii. 7–14","inc":"And the Lord said … unto his people.","ref":"exodus 32:7-14"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vii. 14–31","inc":"Now about the midst … believed on him.","ref":"john 7:14-31"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I waited patiently for the Lord: and he inclined unto me and heard my calling. And he hath put a new song in my mouth: even a thanksgiving unto our God."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to look favourably upon the offerings of thy faithful people, that the oblation made by the reverent service of thy devout servants may be ratified by thy favour. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"We will rejoice in thy salvation, and triumph in the name of the Lord our God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May this holy food and this cup of salvation be profitable to us, O Lord, and both protect us in this transitory life, and obtain for us life eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee to have compassion upon thy people, and mercifully to refresh those who are laden with continual tribulations. Through etc."}]},"q4-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 198","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"When I shall be sanctified in you I will gather you out of all countries, and I will sprinkle clean water upon you, and ye shall be clean from all your filthiness, and a new spirit will I put within you.\nPs. I will alway give thanks unto the Lord: his praise shall ever be in my mouth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who both givest to the just the rewards of their merits, and to sinners their pardon through fasting, have mercy upon thy suppliants, that the confession of our guilt may obtain the remission of our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Ezek. xxxvi. 23–28","inc":"And 1 will sanctify … will be your God.","ref":"ezekiel 36:23-28"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Come, ye children, and hearken unto me: I will teach you the fear of the Lord.\n℣. They had an eye unto him and were lightened: and their faces were not ashamed."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who mortify ourselves in this appointed fast may also be gladdened by holy devotion, and that subduing all earthly affections we may the more readily receive heavenly gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. i. 16–19","inc":"Wash you … good of the land.","ref":"isaiah 1:16-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Blessed are the people, whose God is the Lord: blessed are the folk that he hath chosen to him to be his inheritance.\n℣. By the word of the Lord were the heavens made: and all the hosts of them by the breath of his mouth."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Bring thou me out of my troubles etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John ix. 1–38","inc":"And as Jesus passed by … worshipped him.","ref":"john 9:1-38"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O praise our Lord God, ye people, and make the voice of his praise to be heard; who holdeth our soul in life, and suffereth not our feet to slip. Praised be the Lord, who hath not cast out my prayer: nor turned his mercy from me.\n℣. O be joyful in God all ye lands: sing praises unto the honour of his name, make his praise to be glorious."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We humbly beseech thee, almighty God, that our sins may be cleansed away by this sacrifice; forasmuch as then thou dost bestow on us true health both of mind and body. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord made clay of spittle, and anointed my eyes, and I went and washed, and saw, and believed in God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the sacrament, O Lord our God, which we have received, both fortify us with spiritual food, and protect us with bodily help. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Let thy merciful ears, O Lord, be open to the prayers of thy humble servants; and that they may obtain their petitions, make them to ask such things as shall please thee. Through etc."}]},"q4-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 200","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let the heart of them rejoice that seek the Lord. Seek the Lord and his strength: seek his face evermore.\nPs. O give thanks unto the Lord, and call upon his name: tell the people what things he hath done."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who mortify ourselves in this appointed fast may also be ' P35- ° From St. John ix. ii. gladdened by holy devotion; and that subduing all earthly affections we may the more readily receive heavenly gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"2 Kings iv. 25–38","inc":"So she came … again to Gilgal.","ref":"2 kings 4:25-38"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Look, O Lord, upon the covenant, and forget not the congregation of the poor for ever.\n℣. Arise, O Lord, maintain thine own cause: remember the reproach of thy servants."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John v. 17–29","inc":"But Jesus answered … of damnation.","ref":"john 5:17-29"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Make haste, O Lord, to help me. Let them be driven backward, and put to rebuke, that wish me evil."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Purify us, O merciful God, that the prayers of thy Church, which are pleasing to thee, being accompanied with pious offerings, may through the purification of our minds become more acceptable. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O Lord, I will make mention of thy righteousness only. Thou, O God, hast taught me from my youth up: forsake me not, O God, in mine old age, when I am gray-headed."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, suffer not the heavenly gifts, which thou hast provided for the saving health of thy faithful people to turn to the condemnation of those that receive them. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O God, the founder and governor of thy people, drive far away the sins by which they are beset, that they may always be well pleasing unto thee, and safe under thy protection. Through etc."}]},"q4-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 202","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let the meditation of my heart be always acceptable in thy sight, O Lord, my strength, and my redeemer.\nPs. The heavens declare the glory of God: and the firmament sheweth his handy-work."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who renewest the world through thy ineffable sacraments, grant, we beseech thee, that thy Church may advance in the observance of thy eternal precepts, and not be left destitute of thy temporal assistance. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"i Kings xvii. 17–24","inc":"And it came to pass … ; mouth is truth.","ref":"1 kings 17:17-24"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"It is better to trust in the Lord: than to put any confidence in man.\n℣. It is better to trust in the Lord: than to put any confidence in princes."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John xi. 1–45","inc":"Now a certain man … believed on him.","ref":"john 11:1-45"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"For thou shalt save the people that are in adversity, O Lord, and shalt bring down the high looks of the proud: for who is God, but thou, O Lord?\n℣. My complaint shall come before him, it shall enter even into his ears."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the gifts which we have offered may purify us, and make thee ever favourable to us. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord, seeing the sisters of Lazarus weeping at the grave, wept in the presence of the Jews, and said: Lazarus, come forth; and he came forth, bound hand and foot, who had been dead four days."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the partaking of this sacrament may both continually free us from our own guilt, and defend us from all adversities. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who are conscious of our own infirmity, and trust in thy mercy, may ever rejoice under thy protection. Through etc."}]},"q4-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after the Fourth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 204","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Ho, every one that thirsteth come ye to the waters saith the Lord, and he that hath no money; come and drink with joy.\nPs. Hear my law, O my people: incline your ears unto the words of my mouth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that our devotion may bring forth fruit through thy grace; for then will our fast profit us, if it be well-pleasing to thy goodness. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. xlix. 8–15","inc":"Thus saith the Lord … not forget thee.","ref":"isaiah 49:8-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The poor committeth himself unto thee, O Lord: for thou art the helper of the friendless.\n℣. Why standest thou so far off, O Lord: and hidest thy face in the needful time of trouble? The ungodly for his own lust doth persecute the poor."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John viii. 12–ao","inc":"Then spake Jesus … not yet come.","ref":"john 8:12-0"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Lord is my strong rock and my defence, my refuge, and my Saviour in whom I will trust."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, favourably to receive our offerings, and mercifully to subdue unto thyself even our rebellious wills. Through"},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord is my shepherd: therefore can I lack nothing. He shall feed me in a green pasture: and lead me forth beside the waters of comfort."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy holy gifts which we have received may purify us, and that by their healing operation they may make thee favourable to us. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"O God who choosest rather to shew mercy than to be angry with those that hope in thee, grant to us worthily to lament the evils which we have committed, that so we may find the favour of thy comfort. Through etc."}]},"q5":{"title":"Mass of the Fifth Sunday in Lent (Passion Sunday)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 205","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Give sentence with me, O God, and defend my cause against the ungodly people: O deliver me from the deceitful and wicked man, for thou art the God of my strength.\nPs. O send out thy light and thy truth that they may lead me: and bring me unto thy holy hill and to thy dwelling."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, almighty God, mercifully to look upon this thy family, that by thy great goodness they may be governed and preserved evermore both in body and soul. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Heb. ix. 11–15","inc":"But Christ being come … inheritance.","ref":"hebrews 9:11-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Deliver me, O Lord, from mine enemies. Teach me to do the thing that pleaseth thee.\n℣. It is he that delivereth me from my cruel enemies, and setteth me up above my adversaries: thou shalt rid me from the wicked man."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Many a time have they fought against me from my youth up."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John viii. 46–59","inc":"Which of you convinceth … out of the temple.","ref":"john 8:46-59"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I will thank thee, O Lord, with an unfeigned heart. O do well unto thy servant, that I may live and keep thy word. O quicken thou me according to thy word, O Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Almighty God, graciously behold the offerings of thy faithful people; and grant that the chain of the world may not hold them captive whom by the passion of thy Son thou didst will to set free in all things. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"This is my body which is given for you: this cup is the new testament in my blood, saith the Lord: do this, as oft as ye shall drink it, in remembrance of me."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Be present with us, we beseech thee, O Lord our God, and defend with thy continual help those whom thou hast refreshed with thy mysteries. Through etc."}]},"q5-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 207","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Be merciful unto me, O Lord, for man goeth about to devour me: he is daily fighting and troubling me.\nPs. Mine enemies are daily in hand to swallow me up: for they be many that fight against me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Sanctify, we beseech thee, O Lord, our fasts, and mercifully bestow upon us the pardon of all our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jonah iii","inc":"And the word of the Lord … did it not.","ref":"jonah 3"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Hear my prayer, O God: and hearken unto the words of my mouth.\n℣. Save me, O God, for thy name's sake: and avenge me in thy strength."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vii. 32–39","inc":"And the Pharisees … should receive.","ref":"john 7:32-39"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Turn thee, O Lord, and deliver my soul: O save me for thy mercy's sake.\n℣. O Lord, rebuke me not in thine indignation: neither chasten me in thy displeasure."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receiving thy safe conduct we offer to thee, O Lord, the sacrifice of praise, beseeching thee that we may more safely and truly receive the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord of Hosts, he is the king of glory."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the partaking of the sacrament of salvation may both cleanse and heal us. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, unto thy people the spirit of truth and peace, that they may know thee with all their mind, and work with pious devotion the things which are well-pleasing unto thee. Through etc."}]},"q5-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 208","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O tarry thou the Lord's shall comfort thine heart; and leisure: be strong and he put thou thy trust in the Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that our fast may be acceptable unto thee; and that by purifying us, it may make us worthy of thy grace, and lead to eternal recovery. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Bel and the Dragon 29–42","inc":"So they came … den of lions.","ref":"daniel 14:29-42"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Defend my cause, O Lord: O deliver me from the deceitful and wicked man.\n℣. O send out thy light and thy truth that they may lead me, and bring me unto thy holy hill."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vii. 1–13","inc":"After these things Jesus … fear of the Jews.","ref":"john 7:1-13"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"And they that know thy name will put their trust in thee: for thou, Lord, hast never failed them that seek thee. O praise the Lord, which dwelleth in Sion; for he forgetteth not the complaint of the poor.\n℣. Thou art set in the throne that judgest right. Thou hast rebuked the heathen, and destroyed the ungodly. Minister true judgment unto thy people, and be a defence for the oppressed."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We present this sacrifice to be offered to thee, O Lord; that it may obtain for us temporal consolation, and that we may with more certainty hope for thy eternal promises. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Deliver me, O God of Israel, out of all my troubles."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we being continually occupied in divine things, may be made worthy to draw nigh to thy heavenly gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant to us, Lord, we beseech thee, as a company of servants to persevere in doing thy will, that in our days thy people that serveth thee may be increased both in worth and number. Through etc."}]},"q5-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 210","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"It is he that delivereth me from my cruel enemies, and setteth me up above mine adversaries, thou shalt rid me from the wicked man, O Lord.\nPs. I will love thee, O Lord, my strength: the Lord is my stony rock and my defence."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Enlighten, O merciful God, the hearts of thy faithful people by this holy fast, and graciously give ear unto their supplications, unto whom thou hast given an hearty desire to pray. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Levit. xix. 11–19","inc":"Ye shall not steal … keep my statutes.","ref":"leviticus 19:11-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"I will magnify thee, O Lord, for thou hast set me up: and not made my foes to triumph over me.\n℣. O Lord my God, I cried unto thee, and thou hast healed me. Thou, Lord, hast brought my soul out of hell: thou hast kept my life from them that go down into the pit."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Bring thou me out of my troubles etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John x. 22–38","inc":"And it was at Jerusalem … I in him.","ref":"john 10:22-38"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Deliver me from mine enemies, O God: defend me, O Lord, from them that rise up against me.\n℣. For, lo, they lie waiting for my soul: and the mighty men are gathered against me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, merciful God, that we may offer unto thee, with unfeigned obedience, the sacrifice of propitiation and praise. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"I will wash my hands in innocency, O Lord, and so will I go to thine altar; that I may shew the voice of thanksgiving, and tell of all thy wondrous works."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Having received the blessing of the heavenly gift, we humbly beseech thee, almighty God, that the same may produce in us the effect of the sacrament, and procure our salvation. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Lord, we beseech thee, that thy hoped for mercy may come to thy people which prayeth; and of thy heavenly bounty grant unto them both to ask such things as be rightful, and also to obtain their petitions. Through etc."}]},"q5-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 212","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"According to true judgment hast thou done all things that thou hast done unto us, O Lord, for we have sinned against thee, and have not obeyed thy commandments; but give glory unto thy name, and deal with us according to the multitude of thy mercies.\nPs. Great is the Lord, and worthy to be praised: in the city of our God, even upon his holy hill."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the dignity of human nature, which hath been wounded by excess, may be cured by the process of healing moderation. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Song of the Three Children 12–22","inc":"And cause not … the whole world.","ref":"daniel 3:35-45"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Bring presents, and come into his courts: O worship the Lord in the beauty of holiness.\n℣. The Lord discovereth the thick bushes: in his temple doth every man speak of his honour."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vii. 40–53","inc":"Many of the people … his own house.","ref":"john 7:40-53"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"By the waters of Babylon we sat down and wept: when we remembered thee, O Sion.\n℣. Remember the children of Edom, O Lord, in the day of"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Who by the fasting of the body etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O think upon thy servant as concerning thy word: wherein thou hast caused me to put my trust. The same is my comfort in my trouble."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, O Lord, that what we have received in our mouths we may take with pure minds, and that the temporal gift may become to us an eternal remedy. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Be favourable, we beseech thee, O Lord, to thy people, that forsaking what displeaseth thee, they may the rather be filled with delight in thy commandments. Through etc."}]},"q5-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 214","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Have mercy upon me, O Lord, for I am in trouble: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies, and from them that trouble me. Let me not be confounded, O Lord, for I have called upon thee.\nPs. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust: let me never be put to confusion, deliver me in thy righteousness."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O Lord, we beseech thee, graciously pour into our hearts the help of thy grace: that we subduing our sins by voluntary discipline, may rather mortify ourselves in this life, than be consigned to eternal punishment. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jer. xvii. 13–18","inc":"O Lord, all that forsake … double destruction.","ref":"jeremiah 17:13-18"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Mine enemies speak peacefully to me, and in wrath they were grievous to me.\n℣. This thou hast seen, O Lord, my God: hold not thy tongue then, go not far from me, O Lord."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John xi. 47–54","inc":"Then gathered … with his disciples.","ref":"john 11:47-54"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Blessed art thou, O Lord; O teach me thy statutes, and give me not over to my proud oppressors. So shall I make answer unto my blasphemers.\n℣. It grieveth me when I see the transgressors: O Lord, when wilt thou be avenged of them that persecute me?"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant unto us, O merciful God, that we may always worthily serve at thy altar, and be saved by continual communion thereat. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Deliver me not over into the will of mine adversaries: for there are false witnesses risen up against me, and such as speak wrong."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the perpetual protection of the sacrifice which we have received never forsake us, O Lord, and ward off from us all things that are hurtful. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Grant to us, Lord, we beseech thee, the pardon of our sins, and the increase of our religion; and that thou mayest multiply thy gifts upon us, make us more ready to obey thy commandments. Through etc."}]},"q5-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after the Fifth Sunday in Lent","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 215","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Have mercy upon me, O Lord, for I am In trouble: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies; and from them that persecute me. Let me not be confounded, O Lord, for I have called upon thee.\nPs. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust: let me never be put to confusion, deliver me in thy righteousness."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy people consecrated to thee may increase in the spirit of pious devotion; that being exercised in holy actions they may be the more plenteously fulfilled with thy gifts, as they become more acceptable to thy majesty. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jer. xviii. i8–23","inc":"Then they said … time of thine anger.","ref":"jeremiah 18:18-23"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Mine enemies speak peacefully to me, and in wrath they were grievous to me.\n℣. This thou hast seen, O Lord my God: hold not thy tongue then, go not far from me, O Lord."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vi. 53–71","inc":"Then Jesus said … one of the twelve.","ref":"john 6:53-71"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Remember that I stood before thee, that I might speak good in their behalf, and that I might turn away thine indignation from them."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O almighty Creator, the offering which we with fasting present unto thee, out of the largess of thine own bounty to us; and graciously turn to our eternal welfare that which thou hast provided for our temporal support. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Deliver me not into the will of mine enemies: for there are false witnesses risen up against me, and such as speak wrong."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord our God, that having been filled with the abundance of the divine gifts we may ever live by the participation thereof. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"May thy right hand, O Lord, we beseech thee, protect thy people that prayeth; and worthily purify and teach them, so that they may both find present comfort, and make advance towards the good things to come hereafter. Through etc."}]},"palm":{"title":"Mass: Palm Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 217","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Be not thou far from me O Lord: thou art my succour, haste thee to help me. Save me from the lion's mouth: thou hast heard me also from among the horns of the unicorn.\nPs. My God, my God, look upon me: why hast thou forsaken me?"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, who hast sent our Saviour to take upon him our flesh, and to suffer death upon the cross, that mankind should follow the example of his humility; mercifully grant, that we may both follow the example of his patience, and also be made partakers of his resurrection. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Phil. ii. 5–11","inc":"Let this mind … God the Father.","ref":"philippians 2:5-11"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Thou hast holden me by my right hand: thou shalt guide me with thy counsel; and after that receive me with glory."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"To he sung by the Quire in alternate verses."},{"k":"The Passion","pr":"St. Matt. xxvi. 1 – xxvii. 61","inc":"The Passion of our Lord is sung, and the Gospel follows from where it ends.","ref":"matthew 26|matthew 27:1-61"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mat. xxvi. 62–66","inc":"Now the next day … setting a watch.","ref":"matthew 26:62-66"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Thy rebuke hath broken my heart; I am full of heaviness; I looked for some to have pity on me, but there was no man, neither found I any to comfort me. They gave me gall to eat, and when I was thirsty, they gave me vinegar to drink."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We offer unto thee, O God, Father almighty, the whole burnt offering of the spotless Paschal Lamb; by whose blood, we beseech thee, save us from the ravages of the destroyer, and lead us into thy newly-promised land. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Father, if this cup may not pass away from me, except I drink it, thy will be done."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, look graciously upon thy faithful people, that they calling to mind again the beginning of their redemption, may profit more and more through those things by the gift of which thou hast refreshed them. Through etc."}]},"hw1":{"title":"Mass: Monday in Holy Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 230","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Plead thou my cause, O Lord, with them that strive with me: and fight thou against them that fight against me. Lay hand upon the shield and buckler, and stand up to help me, O Lord, thou strength of my salvation.\nPs. Bring forth the spear, and stop the way against them that persecute me."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, Almighty God, that we who by reason of our weakness, faint under so many adversities, may recover by pleading the passion of thy only-begotten Son. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. l. 5–10","inc":"The Lord God hath opened … upon his God.","ref":"isaiah 50:5-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Awake, O Lord, and stand up to judge my quarrel: avenge thou my cause my God and my Lord."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, deal not with us etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John xii. 1–36","inc":"Then Jesus six days … himself from them.","ref":"john 12:1-36"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Deliver me, O Lord, from mine enemies; for I flee unto thee to hide me. Teach me to do the thing that pleaseth thee, for thou art my God.\n℣. Hearken unto me for thy truth and righteousness sake. And enter not into judgment with thy servant, O Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May this oblation itself commend thy faithful people to thy majesty, O Lord; which through thy Son our Lord Jesus Christ hath reconciled them that were at enmity. Who etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Let them be put to confusion and shame together, that rejoice at my trouble: let them be clothed with rebuke and dishonour, that boast themselves against me."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Having been filled with thy saving gift we beseech thy mercy, O Lord, that by this same sacrament, by which thou makest us to grow in this life, thou wouldest make us partakers of the life eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"Help us, O God of our salvation, and grant that we may come with joy to the commemoration of those benefits by which thou hast deigned to restore us. Through etc."}]},"hw2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday in Holy Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 231","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"But we ought to glory in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, in whom is our salvation, life, and resurrection, through whom we are saved and freed.\nPs. God be merciful unto us and bless us: and shew us the light of his countenance, and be merciful unto us."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, grant us so to celebrate the sacrament of the Lord's passion, that we may be found worthy to obtain pardon. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Jer. xi. i8–20","inc":"The Lord hath given … revealed my cause.","ref":"jeremiah 11:18-20"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Nevertheless, when they were sick, I put on sackcloth, and humbled my soul with fasting: and my prayer shall turn into mine own bosom."},{"k":"The Passion","pr":"St. Mark xiv. 1 – xv. 41","inc":"The Passion of our Lord is sung, and the Gospel follows from where it ends.","ref":"mark 14|mark 15:1-41"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark xv. 42–46","inc":"And now when the even … the sepulchre.","ref":"mark 15:42-46"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Keep me, O Lord, from the hands of the ungodly: and preserve me from the wicked men, O Lord.\n℣. Who are purposed to overthrow my goings. The proud have laid a snare for me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Sanctify, we beseech thee, O Lord, the oblations of a people devoted to thee; that cleansed from the earthly conversation of the old man, we may be renewed by progress in a heavenly life. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"They that sit in the gate speak against me: and the drunkards make songs upon me. But, Lord, I make my prayer unto thee: in an acceptable time. Hear me, O God, in the multitude of thy mercy."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Having been filled with the grace of thy holy gift, we humbly beseech thee, O Lord, that what in adoration we have thought sweet to our bodily taste, we may perceive to be more exceeding sweet to our souls. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"May thy pity, O God, protect us from all gradual surrender to the old man, and make us capable of a new and holy life. Through etc."}]},"hw3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday in Holy Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 233","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"At the name of the Lord every knee should bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth; because the Lord became obedient unto death even the death of the cross: therefore Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.\nPs. Lord hear my prayer, and let my crying come unto thee."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who are continually punished for our offences, may be delivered by the passion of thy Only-begotten one. Who etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. lxii. 11; lxiii. 1–7","inc":"Say ye to … bestowed on us.","ref":"isaiah 62:11|isaiah 63:1-7"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Hide not thy face from thy servant, for I am in trouble: O haste thee, and hear me.\n℣. Save me, O God; for the waters are come in, even unto my soul. I stick fast in the deep mire, where no ground is."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who hast willed that thy Son should suffer death upon the cross for us, that thou mightest drive away from us the power of the enemy, grant unto us thy servants that we may obtain the grace of his resurrection. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Is. liii. 1–10, 12","inc":"Who hath believed … for the transgressors.","ref":"isaiah 53:1-10|isaiah 53:12"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Hear my prayer, O Lord: and let my cry come unto thee."},{"k":"The Passion","pr":"St. Luke xxii. 1 – xxiii. 49","inc":"The Passion of our Lord is sung, and the Gospel follows from where it ends.","ref":"luke 22|luke 23:1-49"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke xxiii. 50–53","inc":"And, behold, there was a man named Joseph … never man before was laid.","ref":"luke 23:50-53"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Hear my prayer, O Lord: and let my crying come unto thee.\n℣. Hide not thy face from me: hide not thy face from me."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the gift we offer, and so work in us, that we may apprehend with pious affections that which by the mystery of the passion of thy Son our Lord we do set forth. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"I mingled my drink with weeping; for thou hast taken me up and cast me down: and I am withered like grass. But thou, O Lord, shalt endure for ever. Thou shalt arise and have mercy upon Sion: for it is time that thou have mercy upon her."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Quicken our understanding, almighty God, that through the death in this world of thy Son, to which these adorable mysteries do testify, we may surely trust that thou hast given to us eternal life. Through etc."},{"k":"Prayer over the people","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to behold this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ was contented to be betrayed, and given up into the hands of wicked men, and to suffer death upon the cross. Who now etc."}]},"hw4":{"title":"Mass: Maundy Thursday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 236","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"But we ought to glory in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, in whom is our salvation, life, and resurrection, through whom we have been saved and freed.\nPs. God be merciful unto us, and bless us: and shew the light of his countenance upon us, and be merciful unto us."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, from whom both the traitor Judas received the punishment of his crime, and the robber the reward of his confession, grant unto us the full effect of thy propitiation, that as in his passion our Lord Jesus Christ rendered unto each according to their different deservings, so having destroyed the old man in us, he may grant unto us the grace of his resurrection. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. xi. 20–32","inc":"When ye come together … with the world.","ref":"1 corinthians 11:20-32"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Christ became obedient for us unto death, even the death of the cross."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xiii. 1–15","inc":"Now before the feast … have done to you.","ref":"john 13:1-15"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The right hand of the Lord hath the pre-eminence: the right hand of the Lord bringeth mighty things to pass. I shall not die but live, and declare the works of the Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, holy Father, almighty eternal God, that he himself may make this sacrifice acceptable to thee, who, according to to-day's tradition set forth this to be done by his disciples, in remembrance of him, Jesus Christ our Lord. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord Jesus, after he had supped with his disciples, washed their feet and saith unto them. Know ye what I have done unto you? I your Lord and master have given you an example, that ye too should do as I have done."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord our God, that having been refreshed with life-giving food, we may, through thy gift of immortality, obtain that which we follow after in this time of our mortal life. Through etc."}]},"hw5":{"title":"Mass: Good Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 251","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Hosea, vi. 1–6","inc":"Come and let us return … burnt offerings.","ref":"hosea 6:1-6"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O Lord, have heard thy speech and was afraid; I considered thy works, and was astonished."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, from whom both the traitor Judas received the punishment of his crime, etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Exod. xii. 1–11","inc":"And the Lord spake … the Lord's passover.","ref":"exodus 12:1-11"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Deliver me, O Lord, from the evil man; and preserve me from the wicked man."},{"k":"The Passion","pr":"St. John xviii. 1 – xix. 37","inc":"The Passion of our Lord is sung, and the Gospel follows from where it ends.","ref":"john 18|john 19:1-37"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xix. 38–42","inc":"And after this Joseph … nigh at hand.","ref":"john 19:38-42"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray, most dearly beloved unto us, first of all for the holy Church of God, that our God and Lord would vouchsafe to preserve it in peace throughout the whole world, subjecting to it principalities and powers; and that he would grant unto us, that we leading a quiet and peaceable life may glorify God the Father almighty."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, who hast revealed thy glory to all nations in Christ, preserve, we beseech thee, the works of thine own mercy, that thy Church which is spread throughout the whole world may persevere with steadfast faith in the confession of thy name. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray also for our most blessed Pope N., that our God and Lord who hath chosen him into the order of the Episcopate may preserve him in health and safety for his holy Church, to rule the holy people of God."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, by whose counsel all things are established, mercifully regard our prayers, and of thy goodness preserve the Prelate chosen for us, that the Christian people which is governed by such authority may increase in meritorious faith under so great a Pontiff. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, by whose Spirit the whole body of the Church is governed and sanctified, hearken unto our supplication for all orders of men; that by the gift of thy grace every member of the same may faithfully serve thee. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray for our most Christian King N., that our Lord and God may make all barbarous nations subject to him, for our perpetual peace."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray also for our Catechumens, that our Lord and God may open the ears of their hearts, and the door of his mercy; that, having received the remission of all their sins through the laver of regeneration, they may themselves also be found worthy in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray, most dearly beloved unto us, to God the Father almighty, that he would purge the world of all errors, remove diseases, drive away famine, open prisons, loose chains, grant a safe return to travellers, health to the sick, and a haven of safety to such as are at sea."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, the consolation of the sorrowful, the strength of those that travail, let the prayers of such as call upon thee in any manner of tribulation come unto thee; that all may rejoice that thy mercy hath been present with them in all their necessities. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray also for Heretics and Schismatics, that our God and Lord Jesus Christ would deliver them from all errors, and would vouchsafe to recall them to their holy mother, the Catholic and Apostolic Church."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, who savest all men, and wouldest not that any should perish, look on those souls which have been deceived by the fraud of the devil; that, laying aside all malice and heresy, the hearts of the erring may learn wisdom, and return to the unity of thy truth. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray also for the perfidious Jews, that our God and Lord would take away the veil from their hearts, that they themselves also may acknowledge Jesus Christ our Lord. Let us pray. Let us kneel, and Rise are not said here."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, who dost not reject from thy mercy even the perfidious Jews, hear our prayers which we offer unto thee for the blindness of that people, that, acknowledging the light of thy truth which is Christ, they may be brought out of their darkness. Through etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let us pray also for Pagans, that almighty God would remove iniquity from their hearts; that, leaving their idols they may be converted to the living and true God, and his only Son, Jesus Christ our God and Lord, with whom and with the Holy Ghost God liveth and reigneth, world without end."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, who wouldest not the death of sinners, but ever seekest their life, mercifully receive our prayer, and deliver them from the worship of idols, and gather them into thy holy Church, to the praise and glory of thy name. Through etc."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to behold this thy family, for which our Lord Jesus Christ was contented to be betrayed into the hands of wicked men, and to suffer death upon the cross. Who now etc."}]},"hw6":{"title":"Mass: Easter Even","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 265","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Collect","tx":"May thy bless + ing, O Lord, descend upon this species of incense or frankincense, as on that of which thy prophet David sang, saying, Let my prayer be set forth in thy sight as the incense; let it be unto us an odour of comfort, sweetness, and grace; that by its fumes every illusion of the enemy, of mind and body, may be put to flight; that we may be, in the words of the apostle Paul, a sweet savour unto God. Before the face of this incense or frankincense let every assault of devils flee away, as dust before the face of the wind, and as smoke before the face of the fire. And grant, most holy Father, that this incense of sweet savour may abide for ever for the aid of thy Church, and for the cause of religion, that by mystic signification the fragrant odour may shew forth to us the sweetness of spiritual virtues. Therefore, we beseech thee, almighty God, by the illimitable right hand of thy majesty, that thou wouldst deign to ble + ss this creature compounded of a mixture of various things, that wherever the smoke of its sweet scent shall spread abroad, it may miraculously prevail, in virtue of thy holy name, to put to flight all phantastic assaults of unclean spirits, and to drive away all diseases, and to restore health, and with most fragrant odour to diffuse its sweet smell before thee for evermore. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"I will sing unto the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously; the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea. The Lord is my strength and my song, and he is become my salvation."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, whose ancient miracles we perceive to cast their beams even on our age; because while thou didst stretch out the right hand of thy power to deliver one people from Egyptian persecution, thou dost turn it to the working out of the salvation of the gentiles through the water of regeneration; grant, we beseech thee, that the fulness of the whole world may become the sons of Abraham, and succeed to the dignity of Israel. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"My well-beloved hath a vineyard in a very fruitful hill."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, who in the pages of both testaments hast instructed us in the celebration of the Paschal sacrament, give unto us such a sense of thy mercies, that out of our reception of thy present gifts there may arise a firm expectation of gifts to come. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Give ear, O ye heavens, and I will speak; and hear, O earth, the words of my mouth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, who dost ever multiply thy Church by the calling of the gentiles, mercifully grant that those who are washed by thee in the water of baptism may be defended by thy continual protection. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Like as the hart desireth the water-brooks, so longeth my soul after thee, O God.\n℣. My soul is athirst for God, yea, even for the living God: when shall I come to appear before the presence of God '. tJ. My tears have been my meat day and night; while they daily say unto me. Where is now thy God?"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"} O God, who dost enlighten this most holy night with the glory of the Lord's resurrection, preserve in the children of thy new family the spirit of adoption which thou hast given, that being renewed both in body and mind they may offer unto thee a pure service. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Col. iii. 1–4","inc":"If ye then be risen … in glory.","ref":"colossians 3:1-4"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O praise the Lord, all ye heathen: praise him, all ye nations."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt, xxviii. 1–7","inc":"In the end of the sabbath … I have told you.","ref":"matthew 28:1-7"},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the prayers of thy people with the oblation of these sacrifices, that being consecrated in the Paschal mysteries, they may by thy help avail to thy eternal welfare. Through etc."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Alleluya. Alleluya. Alleluya."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Pour upon us, O Lord, the spirit of thy love, that they whom thou hast satisfied with the Paschal sacrament, may through thy goodness be made to be of one mind. Through etc."}]},"easter":{"title":"Mass: Easter Day","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 288","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"When I wake up I am present with thee, alleluya; thou hast laid thine hand upon me, alleluya; such know is too wonderful for me, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O Lord, thou hast searched me out and known me: thou knowest my down sitting and mine uprising."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, who through thine only-begotten One, hast on this day overcome death, and opened unto us the gate of everlasting life; as by prevention thou dost put into our minds good desires, so by thy help bring the same to good effect. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. v. 7, 8","inc":"Purge out therefore … sincerity and truth.","ref":"1 corinthians 5:7-8"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"This day the dawn glows bright above the sun,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark, xvi. 1–7","inc":"Mary Magdalene and … as he said unto you.","ref":"mark 16:1-7"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The earth trembled and was still, when God arose to judgment. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the prayers of thy people, together with the offerings of the sacrifice, that being consecrated in these Paschal mysteries, they may, by thy assistance, avail to our eternal healing. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Ci"},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Pour into us, O Lord, the spirit of thy love; that those whom thou hast fed with the Paschal sacrament, may by thy goodness be made of one mind. Through etc."}]},"e0-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 296","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The Lord hath brought you into a land flowing with milk and honey, alleluya: and that the law of the Lord may always be in your mouth. Alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O give thanks unto the Lord, for he is gracious: because his mercy endureth for ever."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who in the Paschal feast hast conferred restoration upon the world, continue, we beseech thee, unto thy people this heavenly gift, that they may both be found worthy to attain unto perfect freedom, and also to advance unto life eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, x. 37–43","inc":"That word I say … remission of sins.","ref":"acts 10:37-43"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. Did not our hearts burn within us, while he talked with us by the way, concerning Jesus?"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, xxiv. 13–35","inc":"And, behold, two … breaking of bread.","ref":"luke 24:13-35"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The angel of the Lord descended from heaven, and said unto the women, Whom seek ye? He is risen, as he said. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, as we offer this Paschal sacrifice, that what we celebrate with outward act, we may comprehend with inward effect, through him who rose from the dead, our Lord Jesus Christ, thy Son, Who etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Lord is risen, and hath appeared unto Peter."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the devout partaking of the Paschal sacrament may have its perfect working in us, and may win our thoughts from earthly affections to the heavenly ordinance. Through etc."}]},"e0-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 300","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"She shall give him the water of wisdom to drink."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"He shall be stayed upon her, and shall not be moved. Alleluya. She shall exalt him for ever. Alleluya."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Ps. O give thanks unto the Lord, for he is gracious: because his mercy endureth for ever."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who dost continually multiply thy church with new offspring, grant unto thy servants that they may hold fast in their lives the sacrament which they have received by faith. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, xiii. 26–33","inc":"Men and brethren … Jesus again.","ref":"acts 13:26-33"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it.\n℣. Let them give thanks whom the Lord hath redeemed, and delivered them from the hand of the enemy, and gathered them out of the lands."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Jesus our Lord rose, and stood in the midst of his disciples, and said. Peace be unto you."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"p\nPour forth, chaste band, your holy canticles,\nWith deep-toned organ-peal accompanied;\nUnto the King who burst the gates of hell,\nOur God, repeat your joyful melodies.\n\"When death he had o'ercome, he rose again,\nBearing perpetual joy to all the world.\nLost souls, that haunt Cocytus' dismal brink.\nUnwonted brightness wondering descry\nAs he doth enter, blessed Lord of life.\nThe mighty demon multitude.\nSmitten with fear and trembling, quake;\nDeeply they sigh and wail aloud,\nAnd much they marvel, who so bold\nTo break the iron prison-bars.\nMeanwhile into the courts above.\nBegirt with glittering bands he comes,\nAnd comforts the disciples' fainting hearts.\nLet us awe-struck his trophies contemplate,\nAnd with low voice our supplication make.\nThat we amidst the honour'd virgin band\nMay celebrate our Easter Festival,\nAnd on the hallow'd Galilaean day\nGaze on the glorious beams of dawning light."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, xxiv. 36–47","inc":"Jesus himself stood … among all nations.","ref":"luke 24:36-47"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Lord thundered out of heaven, and the Highest gave his thunder. The springs of the waters were seen. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O Lord, the prayers of thy church, which rejoiceth in the grace of its redemption, that it may be sustained by confidence in these present gifts, and by faith in thy resurrection. Who livest, etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"If ye be risen with Christ, seek those things which are above, alleluya, where Christ sitteth on the right hand of God. Set your affection on things above. Alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the mysteries of which we have partaken may sanctify us, and enable us duly to observe the Paschal feast. Through etc."}]},"e0-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 302","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Come ye blessed of my Father, receive the kingdom, alleluya, which is prepared for you from the beginning of the world. Alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Sing unto the Lord a new song: for he hath done marvellous things."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"0 God, who makest us glad by the yearly solemnity of the resurrection of our Lord, mercifully grant, that through the feasts which we keep in this world, we may be made worthy to attain unto the joys of the world to come. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, iii. 12, 13–15, 17–19","inc":"And when Peter … blotted out.","ref":"acts 3:12-15|acts 3:17-19"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let all the world with prayer and praise"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xxi. 1–14","inc":"After these things … risen from the dead.","ref":"john 21:1-14"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Lord opened the doors of heaven. He rained down manna also upon them for to eat, and gave them food from heaven. So man did eat angel's food. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May the sacrifices, O Lord, offered in the Paschal rejoicings obtain for us the gift of thy gracious favour, by which thy church is wonderfully fed and nourished. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more, alleluya. Death hath no more dominion over him, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the reception of thy adorable sacrament may both cleanse us from the old man, and change us into a new creature. Through etc."}]},"e0-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 304","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"They magnified with one accord thine hand that fought for them, alleluya: for wisdom opened the mouth of the"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who hast united divers nations in the confession of thy name, grant that those who have been born again in the baptismal font may be one both in inward faith and in outward devotion. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, viii. 26–40","inc":"And the angel … to Caesarea.","ref":"acts 8:26-40"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"On the day of my resurrection, saith the Lord, I will go before you into Galilee."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"3 %\"\nSay, our home revisiting,\nFrom what region dost thou spring,\nTo the world new joys to bring?\nWith clear voice and placid eyes,\n\" Alleluia! she replies,\n\" I declare high mysteries;\nI have heard an angel cry,\nChrist, the Lord of stars on high.\nHath arisen gloriously."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xx. 11–18","inc":"But Mary stood … these things unto her.","ref":"john 20:11-18"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"On the day of your feast, saith the Lord, I will bring you into a land flowing with milk and honey."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Graciously accept, we beseech thee, O Lord, the gifts of thy people, that having been renewed by the confession of thy name in baptism, they may obtain everlasting felicity. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Ye, his peculiar people, shew forth the praises of him, alleluya, who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"' Partly from Ex. iii. 8. i Pet. ii. 9."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Hear our prayers, O Lord, that the reception of the sacred mysteries of our redemption may obtain for us help in this life present, and win for us the joys of the life everlasting. Through etc."}]},"e0-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 307","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The Lord brought them out safely, alleluya, and overwhelmed their enemies with the sea. Alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Hear my law, O my people: incline your ears unto the words of my mouth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, who hast given unto us the Paschal sacrament in the covenant of the reconciliation of mankind, grant that what we profess in our minds we may practise in our lives. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I St. Peter, iii. 18–22","inc":"For Christ also hath once … right hand of God.","ref":"1 peter 3:18-22"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"YJ. Tell it out among the heathen that the Lord hath reigned from the tree."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"..V 1 Unto the Paschal Victim bring,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt, xxviii. i6–20","inc":"Then the eleven disciples … end of the world.","ref":"matthew 28:16-20"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"This shall be a day to be remembered by you, alleluya, and ye shall keep it for a solemn feast-day for the Lord in your generations, a day for a statute for ever, alleluya, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, graciously receive the offerings which we present for an atonement for the sins of those who have been born again, and for the hastening of thy heavenly succour. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth, alleluya. Go ye, teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"O God, who by the remission of sins hast begotten the children of thy adoption, grant that all thy faithful may truly apprehend that which they have received in this present sacrament. Through etc."}]},"e0-6":{"title":"Mass: Saturday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 309","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"He brought forth his people with joy, alleluya, and his chosen with gladness, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O give thanks unto the Lord, and call upon his name: tell the people what things he hath done."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who have celebrated with reverence this Paschal feast, may through it be found worthy to arrive at everlasting joys. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"i St. Pet. ii. 1–10","inc":"Wherefore laying aside … obtained mercy.","ref":"1 peter 2:1-10"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"'iJ. This is the day which the Lord hath made: we will rejoice and be glad in it."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"of Alleluya."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xx. 1–9","inc":"The first day of the week … from the dead.","ref":"john 20:1-9"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Blessed be he that cometh in the name of the Lord: we have wished you good luck, ye that are of the house of the Lord. God is the Lord, who hath shewed us light. Alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we may always shew forth our gratitude by these Paschal mysteries; that the work of restoration being continually carried on in us, may be unto us a cause of perpetual gladness. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"As many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ. Alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that we may be quickened by the gift of our redemption, and that through this help to eternal salvation, a true faith may ever be increased in us. Through etc."}]},"e1":{"title":"Mass: Sunday in the Octave of Easter (Low Sunday)","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 312","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"When I wake up I am present with thee, alleluya; thou hast laid thine hand upon me, alleluya; such know is too wonderful for me, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O Lord, thou hast searched me out and known me: thou knowest my down sitting and mine uprising."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, who through thine only-begotten One, hast on this day overcome death, and opened unto us the gate of everlasting life; as by prevention thou dost put into our minds good desires, so by thy help bring the same to good effect. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. v. 7, 8","inc":"Purge out therefore … sincerity and truth.","ref":"1 corinthians 5:7-8"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Christ our Passover is sacrificed for us."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"The angel of the Lord descended from heaven, and camel and 1 rolled back the stone from the door, and sat upon it.i"},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Let us with lowly tone\nThe Saviour's praises sing;\nMessiah on his heavenly throne\nDevoutly worshipping;\nWho deigned in flesh to shade\nHis glorious deity;"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark, xvi. 1–7","inc":"Mary Magdalene and … as he said unto you.","ref":"mark 16:1-7"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The earth trembled and was still, when God arose to judgment. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the prayers of thy people, together with the offerings of the sacrifice, that being consecrated in these Paschal mysteries, they may, by thy assistance, avail to our eternal healing. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"as. on Easter Day."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Pour into us, O Lord, the spirit of thy love; that those whom thou hast fed with the Paschal sacrament, may by thy goodness be made of one mind. Through etc."}]},"e1w":{"title":"Mass: Sunday Mass for the week after Low Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 315","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"As new-born babes, alleluya, as reasonable beings desire the sincere milk of the word. Alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Sing we merrily unto God our strength: make a cheerful noise unto the God of Jacob."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who have fulfilled the Paschal feast, may by thy bounty hold it fast in our life and actions. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I St. John, v. 4–10","inc":"Whatsoever is born … witness in himself.","ref":"1 john 5:4-10"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. After eight days Jesus came, the doors being shut, and stood in the midst of his disciples, and said. Peace be unto you."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. The angel of the Lord descended from heaven, and came and rolled back the stone, and sat upon it."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xx. 19–31","inc":"The same day … through his name.","ref":"john 20:19-31"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The angel of the Lord descended from heaven, and said unto the women. Whom seek ye? He is risen, as he said.s Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the gifts of thy glad Church; and as thou hast given her cause for so great joy, grant her also the fruition of everlasting felicity. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Reach hither thy hand, and behold the place of the nails, alleluya; and be not faithless, but believing, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord our God, that thou wouldest make those holy mysteries, which thou hast bestowed upon us for the protection of our renewed life, to be unto us a healing remedy, both now and evermore. Through etc."}]},"e1-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 317","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. xv. 12–23","inc":"Now if Christ … in his own order.","ref":"1 corinthians 15:12-23"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark, xvi. 9–13","inc":"Now when Jesus … believed they them.","ref":"mark 16:9-13"}]},"e1-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 317","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Heb. xiii. 17–21","inc":"Obey them … and ever. Amen.","ref":"hebrews 13:17-21"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt. xxviii. 8–15","inc":"And they departed … until this day.","ref":"matthew 28:8-15"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"shall be one of the following: after which those for Easter week shall be recommenced viz., those for the third, fourth, and fifth days of the week in order. When these have"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"YJ. Abide with us, O Lord, for it is toward evening, and the day is far spent."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. It was fitting that Christ should suffer, and rise from the dead, and thus enter into his glory."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more; death hath no more dominion over him."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. The most high hath risen from the grave, who hung for us upon the tree."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. Christ hath risen, and hath shined on his people, whom he himself hath redeemed with his blood."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. Christ died for our sins, and rose again for our justification."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"IT. Jesus our Lord rose, and stood in the midst of his disciples, and said, Peace be unto you."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. The Lord rose, and meeting the women, said, All hail: then they came and held him by the feet."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"YJ. On the day of my resurrection, saith the Lord, I will go before you into Galilee."}]},"e2":{"title":"Mass of the Second Sunday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 318","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The earth is full of the goodness of the Lord, alleluya: by the word of the Lord were the heavens made, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Rejoice in the Lord, O ye righteous: for it becometh well the just to be thankful."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who by the humiliation of thy Son hast raised a prostrate world, grant unto thy faithful people perpetual joy, and cause them whom thou hast rescued from the calamity of everlasting death, to have the fruition of joys eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I St. Pet. ii. 21–25","inc":"Christ also suffered … of your souls.","ref":"1 peter 2:21-25"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. I am the good shepherd, and know my sheep, and am known of mine."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. The good shepherd hath risen: who hath given his life for his flock."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, x. 11–16","inc":"I am the good … and one shepherd.","ref":"john 10:11-16"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"0 God, thou art my God, early will I seek thee: I will lift up my hands in thy name, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May this sacred oblation ever win for us a saving blessing, and effectually work in us that which in mystery it sheweth forth. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"1 am the good shepherd, alleluya: and I know my sheep, and am known of mine, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty and merciful God, that we who receive thy quickening grace may ever glory in thy gift. Through etc."}]},"e2-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 320","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Pet. i. i8–25","inc":"Forasmuch as ye know … endureth for ever.","ref":"1 peter 1:18-25"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, xxiv. 1–12","inc":"Upon the first day … come to pass.","ref":"luke 24:1-12"}]},"e2-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 320","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Rom. v. 18–21","inc":"As by the offence … Christ our Lord.","ref":"romans 5:18-21"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Matt. ix. 14–17","inc":"Then came to him … both are preserved.","ref":"matthew 9:14-17"}]},"e3":{"title":"Mass of the Third Sunday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 321","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O be joyful in the Lord, all ye lands, alleluya; sing praises unto the honour of his name, alleluya: make his praise to be glorious. Alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Say unto God, O how wonderful art thou in thy works; through the greatness of thy power."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who shewest to them that be in error the light of thy truth, to the intent they may return into the way of righteousness, grant unto all them that are admitted into the fellowship of Christ's religion, that they may eschew those things that are contrary to their profession, and follow all such things as are agreeable to the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Pet. ii. 11–19","inc":"Dearly beloved … is thankworthy.","ref":"1 peter 2:11-19"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. A little while and ye shall not see me, saith the Lord: and again, A little while and ye shall see me, because I go to the Father."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. But I will see you again, and your heart shall rejoice, and your joys no man taketh from you."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. Christ is risen who created all things, and who pitied mankind."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xvi. 16–22","inc":"A little while … taketh from you.","ref":"john 16:16-22"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Praise the Lord, O my soul; while I live will I praise the Lord: yea, as long as I have any being, I will sing praises unto my God."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that through these mysteries it may be granted unto us to bridle earthly desires, and to learn to love things heavenly. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"A little while and ye shall not see me, alleluya; and again, A little while and ye shall see me, because I go to the Father, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Regard thy people, we beseech thee, O Lord, and graciously absolve from the sins of this world, those whom thou hast vouchsafed to renew by eternal mysteries. Through etc."}]},"e3-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 322","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I St. John, ii. 1–8","inc":"These things write I … now shineth.","ref":"1 john 2:1-8"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, iii. 25–36","inc":"Then there arose … abideth on him.","ref":"john 3:25-36"}]},"e3-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 322","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Thess. v. 5–11","inc":"Ye are all the children … edify one another.","ref":"1 thessalonians 5:5-11"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xii. 46–50","inc":"I am come a light … so I speak.","ref":"john 12:46-50"}]},"e4":{"title":"Mass of the Fourth Sunday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 323","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O sing unto the Lord a new song, alleluya; for he hath done marvellous things, alleluya; his righteousness hath he openly shewed in the sight of the heathen, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who makest the faithful to be of one mind, grant unto thy people that they may love the thing which thou commandest, and desire that which thou dost promise, that so among the sundry and manifold changes of the world our hearts may surely there be fixed where true joys are to be found. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James, i. 17–21","inc":"Every good gift … save your souls.","ref":"james 1:17-21"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. I go my way to him that sent me: but because I have said these things unto you, sorrow hath filled your heart."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. I tell you the truth: it is expedient for you that I go away."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. The Lord hath risen indeed, and hath appeared unto Peter."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xvi. 5–15","inc":"I go my way … shew it unto you.","ref":"john 16:5-15"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"0 be joyful in the Lord, all ye lands, be joyful in the Lord, all ye lands: sing praises unto the honour of his name. O come hither and hearken, all ye that fear God, and I will tell you what God hath done for my soul, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"O God, who, when thou hadst risen from the dead, after thy passion, didst return again in greater power unto thy disciples, mercifully grant that this Paschal sacrifice may both reconcile us to thy majesty and make us more prompt to obtain thy grace by good works. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"When the Comforter is come, even the Spirit of truth, he shall reprove the world of sin, and of righteousness, and of judgment, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Help us, O Lord our God, that through those gifts which we have received with faith, we may both be cleansed from sin and delivered from all dangers. Through etc."}]},"e4-3":{"title":"Mass: Wednesday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 324","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James, ii. 1–13","inc":"My brethren, have not … against judgment.","ref":"james 2:1-13"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xvii. 11–15","inc":"Holy Father, keep … them from the evil.","ref":"john 17:1-15"}]},"e4-5":{"title":"Mass: Friday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 324","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James, ii. 24–26","inc":"Ye see then how … is dead also.","ref":"james 2:24-26"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xiii. 33–36","inc":"Little children, yet … follow me afterwards.","ref":"john 13:33-36"}]},"e5":{"title":"Mass of the Fifth Sunday after Easter","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 324","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"With a voice of singing declare ye, and let alleluya be heard: utter it even to the end of the earth; the Lord hath redeemed his people, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O be joyful in the Lord, all ye lands: sing praises unto the honour of his name, make his praise to be glorious."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, from whom all good things do come, grant to us thy humble servants, that by thy inspiration we may think those things that be good, and by thy guiding may perform the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James, i. 22–27","inc":"Be ye doers … unspotted from the world.","ref":"james 1:22-27"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"fc7. Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name: ask and ye shall receive."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"TJ. Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more: death hath no more dominion over him."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xvi. 23–30","inc":"Verily, verily, I say … forth from God.","ref":"john 16:23-30"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O praise our Lord God, ye people; and make the voice of his praise to be heard; who holdeth our soul in life, and suffereth not our feet to slip. Praised be God, who hath not cast out my prayer, nor turned his mercy from me. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, we beseech thee, O Lord, the prayers of thy faithful people, together with these oblations, that through these duties of pious devotion we may pass to our eternal glory. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O sing unto the Lord, alleluya: sing unto the Lord and praise his name; be telling of his salvation from day to day, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant to us, Lord, we beseech thee, that being satisfied with the fulness of the heavenly table, we may both desire those things that be right, and also may obtain our desire. Through etc."}]},"rog1":{"title":"Mass: Rogation Monday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 326","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"So shall he hear my voice out of his holy temple, alleluya; and my complaint shall come before him; it shall enter even into his ears, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. I will love thee, O Lord my strength; the Lord is my stony rock, and my defence, my Saviour."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who in our affliction put our trust in thy goodness, may ever be defended against adversity by thy protection. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"St. James, v. i6–20","inc":"Confess your faults … multitude of sins.","ref":"james 5:16-20"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"U. O give thanks unto the Lord, for he is gracious; because his mercy endureth for ever."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, xi. 5–'13","inc":"Which of you … them that ask him.","ref":"luke 11:5-13"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"I will give great thanks unto the Lord with my mouth, and praise him among the multitude; for he shall stand at the right hand of the poor, to save his soul from unrighteous judges, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May these gifts, O Lord, we beseech thee, both loose the bonds of our wickedness, and win for us the gift of thy mercy. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Ask and ye shall receive; seek and ye shall find; knock and it shall be opened unto you. For every one that asketh receiveth, and he that seeketh findeth, and to him that knocketh it shall be opened, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Further our prayers, we beseech thee, O Lord, with thy gracious favour; that we who have received thy gifts in time of trouble, may by reason of our comfort grow in love to thee. Through etc."}]},"ascEve":{"title":"Mass: Vigil of the Ascension","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 328","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"0 clap your hands together, all ye people: O sing unto God with the voice of melody, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. He shall subdue the people under us; and the nations under our feet."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty Father, that our minds may ever be intent upon that place whither thy only-begotten Son, our Lord, the glorious author of the approaching festival, hath entered in before; and that we may in our conversation attain to that to which by our faith we reach forward. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, iv. 32–35","inc":"And the multitude … as he had need.","ref":"acts 4:32-35"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"V. O clap your hands together, all ye people: O sing unto God with the voice of melody."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xvii. 1–11","inc":"","ref":"john 17:1-11"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Ye men of Galilee, why stand ye gazing up into heaven? This same Jesus, which is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come in like manner as ye have seen him go into heaven, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We humbly present, O Lord, this sacrifice in honour of the adorable ascension of thy Son, for which we are preparing; grant, we beseech thee, that by means of this most holy communion, we may rise through him and with him into heavenly places. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Father, while I was with them, I kept them whom thou gavest me; alleluya. But now I come to thee. I pray not that thou shouldest take them out of the world, but that thou shouldest keep them from the evil; alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that through this sacrament which we have received, our devout affections may thither ascend where Jesus Christ, our Lord, is with thee in the substance of our nature. Who liveth etc."}]},"asc":{"title":"Mass: Ascension Day","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 329","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Ye men of Galilee, why stand ye gazing up into heaven? alleluya. He shall so come in like manner as ye have seen him go into heaven, alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. And while they looked steadfastly toward heaven, as he went up, behold, two men stood by them in white apparel, which also said:"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that like as we do believe thy only-begotten Son, our redeemer, to have ascended unto the heavens, so we may also in mind dwell in heavenly places. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts i. 1–11","inc":"The former treatise … go into heaven.","ref":"acts 1:1-11"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"fcj. God is gone up with a merry noise; and the Lord with the sound of the trump."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Christ, when he ascended up on high, led captivity captive, and received gifts for men."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. The Lord in the holy place of Sinai, when he ascended up on high, led captivity captive."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"The almighty King, victorious, on this day,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark, xvi. 14–20","inc":"Afterwards he appeared … signs following.","ref":"mark 16:14-20"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"God is gone up with a merry noise; and the Lord with the sound of the trump, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Receive, O Lord, the gifts which we offer for the glorious ascension of thy Son; and mercifully grant that we may be delivered from present dangers and may attain unto eternal life. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Sing unto the Lord, who sitteth in the heavens over all from the beginning."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant to us, we beseech thee, almighty and merciful God, that what we perceive to have been received in visible mysteries we may obtain in their invisible efficacy. Through etc."}]},"ascSun":{"title":"Mass: Sunday after Ascension Day","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 332","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Hearken unto my voice, O Lord, when I cry unto thee, alleluya. My heart hath talked of thee. I have sought thy face. Thy face, Lord, will I seek. O hide not thou thy face from me, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. The Lord is my light and my salvation: whom then shall I fear?"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty everlasting God, make us ever both to entertain a devout will towards thee, and to serve thy majesty with sincere hearts. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I St. Peter, iv. 7–11","inc":"Be ye therefore sober … through Jesus Christ.","ref":"1 peter 4:7-11"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. God reigneth over the heathen; God sitteth upon his holy seat."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"YJ. I will not leave you comfortless: I go away and come again unto you, and your heart shall rejoice."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xv. 26–xvi. 4","inc":"When the Comforter … told you of them.","ref":"john 15:26-14"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Praise the Lord, O my soul; while I live will I praise the Lord: yea, as long as I have any being, I will sing praises unto my God, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we may acceptably offer this sacrifice, to become the body and blood of him, whom sitting at the right hand of thy power we assuredly believe to be interceding for us, our Lord Jesus Christ, thy Son. Who with thee etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Father, when I was with them I kept those that thou gavest me, alleluya. But now I come to thee. I pray not that thou wouldest take them out of the world, but that thou wouldest keep them from the evil, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that through this most holy communion we may confidently believe that to be accomplished in the body of the whole Church, which has already been accomplished in her head. Through etc."}]},"pentEve":{"title":"Mass: Vigil of Pentecost","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 334","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who art the glory of the faithful, and the life of the just, who by thy servant Moses hast instructed us also in the measure of sacred song; grant the gift of thy mercy to all nations, by bestowing happiness and removing terror far away; that warnings of punishment may become the instruments of eternal happiness. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"My well-beloved hath a vineyard, etc."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who by the mouth of thy prophets hast taught us to forsake the things of this world, and to press towards things eternal; grant unto thy servants that by thy heavenly inspiration we may be able to fulfil that which we know thee to have commanded. Through etc."},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Like as the hart desireth the water-brooks: so longeth my soul after thee, O God."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who celebrate the feast of the gift of the Holy Spirit, being kindled with heavenly desires, may thirst for the fountain of life, our Lord Jesus Christ, thy Son."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the rays of thy brightness may shine forth upon us, and that the light of thy light may by the illumination of the Holy Spirit strengthen the hearts of those who have been born again through thy grace. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, xix. 1–8","inc":"And it came to pass … kingdom of God.","ref":"acts 19:1-8"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O praise the Lord all ye heathen: praise him all ye nations. ℣. For his merciful kindness is ever more and more towards us: and the truth of the Lord endureth for ever."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xiv. 15–21","inc":"If ye love me … myself to him.","ref":"john 14:15-21"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"When thou lettest thy breath go forth they shall be made, and thou shalt renew the face of the earth: the glorious majesty of the Lord shall endure for ever. Alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, mercifully to give heed to the offerings of thy people; and that they may be rendered acceptable unto thee, may the saving advent of the Holy Spirit purge our consciences. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"In the last day of the feast Jesus stood, and cried, saying. He that believeth in me, as the scripture hath said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. But this spake he of the Spirit, which they that believe on him should receive, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the Holy Spirit may come, and clearly make manifest to us the majesty of thy Son. Through etc."}]},"pent":{"title":"Mass: Whitsunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 339","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The Spirit of the Lord filleth the world, alleluya: and that which containeth all things hath knowledge of the voice, alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Let God arise, and let his enemies be scattered: let them also that hate him flee before him."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"God, who as at this time didst teach the hearts of thy faithful people by the sending to them the light of thy Holy Spirit, grant us by the same Spirit to have a right judgment in all things, and evermore to rejoice in his comfort. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, ii. 1–11","inc":"When the day of Pentecost … works of God.","ref":"acts 2:1-11"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. The Holy Spirit proceeding from the throne illuminated the hearts of the apostles this day with invisible power."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Now may the Holy Spirit's grace\nMake us his own abiding place,\nOur inmost souls to dispossess\nOf spiritual wickedness.\nMost gracious Spirit, light of all.\nOur minds from darkness disenthral:"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, xiv. 23–31","inc":"If a man love me … so I do.","ref":"john 14:23-31"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Stablish the thing, O God, that thou hast wrought in us. For thy temple's sake at Jerusalem, so shall kings bring presents unto thee, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Sanctify, we beseech thee, O Lord, the gifts we offer, and cleanse our hearts by the light of thy Holy Spirit. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Suddenly there came a sound from heaven, as of a rushing mighty wind, where they were sitting, alleluya: and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and spake of the wonderful works of God, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the outpouring of the Holy Spirit cleanse our hearts, O Lord, and render them fruitful by the inward dew of his grace. Through etc."}]},"pent-1":{"title":"Mass: Monday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 341","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"He should have fed them with the finest wheat flour, alleluya: and with honey out of the stony rock should I have satisfied thee, alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Sing we merrily unto God our strength: make a cheerful noise unto the God of Jacob."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"(. O God, who didst give the Holy Ghost unto thine apostles, grant unto thy people an effectual answer to their devout petition, that they to whom thou hast vouchsafed faith may also enjoy thy gift of peace. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, x. 42–48","inc":"And he commanded … name of the Lord.","ref":"acts 10:42-48"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. When thou lettest thy breath go forth, they shall be made: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. The Comforter, which is the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he will teach you all truth."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Now let the sacred quire.\nWith holy symphony,\nThe promised joys sound forth\nIn fulness sent from heaven.\nAssembled in one place,\nThe apostolic band\nAwaits the glorious gifts.\nForthwith a voice divine.\nFilling their hearts with power.\nAttests the Comforter.\nIn every tongue they speak\nSome mighty mystery\nAnd wondrous works of God.\nIn canticles divine\nHis praises to rehearse\nThe assembly ceases not.\nO God of all the earth,\nThee sun and moon do praise;\nThe universal host\nOf heaven in concert join\nTheir voice with waters deep.\nThee sings the genial earth;\nThee all the glittering stars;\nThee ransom'd souls adore,\nRejoicing in thy love."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, iii. i6–21","inc":"God so loved … wrought in God.","ref":"john 3:16-21"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Lord thundered out of heaven, and the highest gave his thunder: and the springs of the waters were seen."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that according to the promise of thy Son, our Lord Jesus Christ, the Holy Ghost may both more abundantly reveal to us the hidden mystery of this sacrifice, and may graciously disclose to us all truth. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Holy Ghost shall teach you, alleluya, whatsoever I have said unto you, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to work fully in us the healing virtue of our redemption, that we may be found worthy to become the dwelling-place of the Holy Ghost. Through etc."}]},"pent-2":{"title":"Mass: Tuesday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 344","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Receive the gift that is given you, and be glad, giving thanks unto God, alleluya, who hath called you to the heavenly kingdom, alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Hear my law, O my people: incline your ears unto the words of my mouth. s"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the power of the Holy Ghost may come upon us, and that he may both mercifully cleanse our hearts, and defend us from all adversity. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Acts, viii. 14–17","inc":"Now when the apostles … the Holy Ghost.","ref":"acts 8:14-17"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. When thou lettest thy breath go forth, they shall be made: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"YJ. Come, Holy Ghost, fill the hearts of thy faithful people, and kindle in them the fire of thy love."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, x. 1–10","inc":"Verily, verily, I say … more abundantly.","ref":"john 10:1-10"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The Lord opened the doors of heaven. He rained down manna also upon them for to eat, and gave them food from heaven. So man did eat angels' food, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the Holy Ghost may descend upon the altar, and that he may both sanctify the gifts of thy people, and mercifully cleanse the hearts of them that receive the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The Spirit which proceedeth from the Father, alleluya, he shall glorify me, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the Holy Ghost may renew our souls, since he himself is the remission of all sins. Through etc."}]},"pent-3":{"title":"Mass: Ember Wednesday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 346","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O God, when thou wentest before thy people, alleluya, making a way for them, alleluya, dwelling among them, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Let God arise, and let his enemies be scattered; let them also that hate him flee before him."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that the Holy Ghost may remove all carnal desires from our minds, and mightily pour into us spiritual gifts. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Wisdom, i. 1–7","inc":"Love righteousness … of the voice.","ref":"wisdom 1:1-7"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. When thou lettest thy breath go forth, they shall be made: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the Holy Ghost the Comforter, which proceedeth from thee, may illumine our minds, and lead us into all truth, as thy Son hath promised. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts. ii. 14–21","inc":"But Peter standing … shall be saved.","ref":"acts 2:14-21"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"V. The apostles began to speak with other tongues the wonderful works of God."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Th illustrious day, when from the throne\nthe fire of God came rushing down\non Christ's assembled band;\nTo enrich their tongues, their hearts to fill;\nto kindred praise invites us still\nof heart, and tongue, and hand.\nChrist on this pentecostal day\nrevisiting without delay\nthe bride, his promise sent;\nAfter the honey's treasured worth,\nthe rock a store of oil gave forth,\nthe rock now permanent.\nFrom Sinai's mount proclaimed the law\ngraven on stone the people saw,\nnot sent in tongues of fire:\nNewness of heart, and quickened mind,\nwith unity of tongue combined,\nThe chosen few inspire.\nO happy, O most festive day!\nwhereon the early founders lay\nthe Church's pediment;\nThe rising Church's first-fruits born\nto life anew this holy morn\nthree thousand souls present.\nThe two loaves by the law ordained,\ntwo people represent, retained\nBy faith's adoptive tie;\nThe head-stone of the corner, set\nbetween the two, together met,\nhath wrought out unity."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, vi. 44–51","inc":"No man cometh … life of the world.","ref":"john 6:44-51"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"My delight shall be in thy commandments which I have loved. My hands also will I lift up unto thy commandments which I have loved, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the Holy Ghost may sanctify the gifts now offered unto thee, and that our partaking of them may guard us from the contagion of all sin. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"My peace I give unto you, alleluya; my peace I leave with you, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the perpetual splendour of thy brightness, O Lord, shine upon us by virtue of this mystery; and may the grace of the Holy Spirit, together with the consubstantial Son, illuminate us. Through etc."}]},"pent-4":{"title":"Mass: Thursday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 350","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"As on Whitsunday without the verses."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty and merciful God, that thy Holy Spirit may come unto us, and dwell in us, and make us to be a temple worthy of his glory. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, viii. 5–8","inc":"Then Philip went down … in that city.","ref":"acts 8:5-8"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. Suddenly there came a sound from heaven, as of a rushing, mighty wind."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Now let the sacred band the Lord's high names expand,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, ix. 1–6","inc":"Then he called … healing everywhere.","ref":"luke 9:1-6"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Stablish the thing, O God, that thou hast wrought in us, for thy temple's sake at Jerusalem: so shall kings bring presents unto thee."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Endue our gifts, O Lord, with the virtue of the Holy Ghost, that he may make discernible to us now and for evermore that which in the present solemnity we hallow to thy name. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Suddenly there was a sound from heaven as of a rushing, mighty wind, where they were sitting, alleluya: and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost, and spake the wonderful works of God, alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that through the operation of the Holy Ghost our vices may be purged away by this heavenly sacrifice, that we may ever be made meet for thy gifts. Through etc."}]},"pent-5":{"title":"Mass: Ember Friday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 352","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O let my mouth be filled with thy praise, alleluya, that I may sing, alleluya: my lips will be fain when I sing unto thee, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. In thee, O Lord, have I put my trust, let me never be put to confusion: but rid me, and deliver me in thy righteousness."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, merciful Lord, unto thy Church, that when it is gathered together by thy Holy Spirit, it may in no way be disturbed by any assault of the enemy. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Acts, ii. 22–28","inc":"Ye men of Israel … thy countenance.","ref":"acts 2:22-28"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"tT. When thou lettest thy breath go forth, they shall be made: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth.,, Alleluya. ℣. The Spirit of the Lord hath filled the world: and that which containeth all things hath knowledge of the voice."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Now let all the Church uniting,\nPraises meet to God reciting,\nTune aloud their grateful songs;\nOn this day the Spirit Holy\nDeigned upon the apostles lowly\nGrace to pour in fiery tongues.\nMay the Comforter indwelling.\nFrom our hearts all sin expelling,\nTake us for his own abode;\nGifts and graces high outpouring.\nIn our breasts devotion storing.\nSo our life shall_£lease our God.\nMay we Alleluias singing,\nThrough all ages ceaseless ringing,\nRaise our acclamations high.\nPraise ascribe and exaltation,\nHonour, might, and adoration.\nUnto God eternally."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, v. 17–26","inc":"And it came to pass … strange things to-day.","ref":"luke 5:17-26"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Praise the Lord, O my soul: while I live will I praise the Lord: yea, as long as I have any being, I will sing praises unto my God, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"May the sacrifice offered in thy presence, O Lord, be consumed by the same divine fire, by which the Holy Ghost influenced the hearts of the disciples of thy Christ. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"The wind bloweth where it listeth, and thou hearest the sound thereof, but canst not tell whence it cometh, and whither it goeth. Alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that by the receiving of this sacrament the vices of our weak nature may be so purged away that we may perceive in ourselves the gift of thy grace promised anew through the Holy Ghost. Through etc."}]},"pent-6":{"title":"Mass: Ember Saturday in Whitsun Week","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 354","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"The love of God is shed abroad in your hearts, alleluya: by his Spirit which dwelleth in you, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. O Lord, God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before thee."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, graciously pour the Holy Spirit into our hearts, by whose wisdom we were created, and by whose providence we are governed. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Joel, ii. 28–32","inc":"I will pour … shall be delivered.","ref":"joel 2:28-32"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. When thou lettest thy breath go forth, they shall be made: and thou shalt renew the face of the earth."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"is not repeated after the verse, but Let us pray is forthwith said."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Let thy Holy Spirit, O Lord, we beseech thee, inflame us with that fire which our Lord Jesus Christ sent on earth, and earnestly desired that it should be kindled. Who liveth etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Levit. xxiii. 9–11, 15–17, 21","inc":"When ye be come into … your generations.","ref":"leviticus 23:9-1|leviticus 23:15-17|leviticus 23:21"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"fcZ. Come, Holy Ghost, fill the hearts of thy faithful people, and kindle in them the fire of thy love."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"0 God, who for the healing of our souls hast ordered our bodies to be mortified by devout fasting, mercifully grant that we may ever faithfully serve thee both in soul and body. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Deut. xxvi. 1–3, 7–11","inc":"When thou art come in … given unto thee.","ref":"deuteronomy 26:1-3|deuteronomy 26:7-11"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"The apostles spake with other tongues the wonderful works of God."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that being taught by this wholesome fast, we may abstain from all vices and more readily obtain thy favour. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Levit. xxvi. 3–13","inc":"If ye walk in … be my people.","ref":"leviticus 26:3-13"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"\"fc7. Suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a mighty, rushing wind."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we may so abstain from carnal feasting, that we may fast in like manner from the assaults of vice. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Song of the Three Children, 26–66","inc":"The angel of the Lord … for ever.","ref":"daniel 3:49-50|daniel 3:47-48|daniel 3:51-58|daniel 3:61-66|daniel 3:71|daniel 3:67-68|daniel 3:74-76|daniel 3:79-87"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Blessed art thou, O Lord God of our fathers, and to be praised for evermore."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who for thy three children didst assuage the flames of fire, mercifully grant that through the coming of thy Holy Spirit we thy servants may not be set on fire by the flames of vice. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Song of the Three Children, 26–66","inc":"The whole city … the Holy Ghost.","ref":"daniel 3:49-50|daniel 3:47-48|daniel 3:51-58|daniel 3:61-66|daniel 3:71|daniel 3:67-68|daniel 3:74-76|daniel 3:79-87"},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"U. O praise the Lord, all ye heathen: praise him, all ye nations."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Now let the sacred band etc."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, iv. 38–43","inc":"And he arose … other cities also.","ref":"luke 4:38-43"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"0 Lord, God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before thee: let my prayer enter into thy presence, O Lord, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Send, we beseech thee, O Lord, the Holy Spirit, that he may both make these present gifts thy sacrament to us, and purify our hearts for the receiving of the same. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"1 will not leave you comfortless: I will come again to you, alleluya, and your heart shall rejoice,i alleluya, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May thy holy mysteries, O Lord, supply in us a divine fervour, that we may find delight both in their celebration and in their effect. Through etc."}]},"trin":{"title":"Mass: Trinity Sunday","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 358","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Blessed be the Holy Trinity, and the undivided Unity; we will praise him because he hath dealt mercifully with us.\nPs. Let us bless the Father and the Son with the Holy Ghost."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, who hast given unto us thy servants grace, by the confession of a true faith, to acknowledge the glory of the eternal Trinity, and in the power of thy majesty to worship the Unity; we beseech thee that thou wouldest keep us steadfast in this faith, and evermore defend us from all adversities. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Rev. iv. 1–10","inc":"After this I looked … for ever and ever.","ref":"revelation 4:1-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Blessed art thou, O Lord, who beholdest the depths, and sittest upon the cherubim. yj. O ye heavens, bless ye God, for he hath dealt mercifully with us."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. Blessed art thou, O Lord God of our fathers, and to be praised for ever."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Blest be the holy Trinity,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John, iii. 1–15","inc":"There was a man … eternal life.","ref":"john 3:1-15"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Blessed be God the Father, and the Only-begotten Son of God: also the Holy Ghost, because he hath dealt mercifully with us."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"Sanctify, we beseech thee, O Lord God, holy Trinity, through the invocation of thy holy name, the offering of this oblation; and through it perfect us ourselves to be an eternal gift unto thee. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"We bless the God of heaven, and we will give thanks unto him in the sight of all that live, because he hath dealt mercifully with US."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"May the reception of this sacrament, O Lord our God, and the confession of the everlasting holy Trinity, and of the undivided Unity of the same, be profitable to our salvation both in body and soul. In which thou livest etc."}]},"corpus":{"title":"Mass: Corpus Christi","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 361","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"He should have fed them also with the finest wheatflour, alleluya, and with honey out of the stony rock should I have satisfied thee/ alleluya, alleluya, alleluya.\nPs. Sing we merrily unto God our strength: make a cheerful noise unto the God of Jacob."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who in a wonderful sacrament hast left us a memory of thy passion, grant us, we beseech thee, so to venerate the sacred mysteries of thy body and blood, that we may ever perceive in ourselves the fruit of thy redemption. Who livest etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"I Cor. xi. 23–29","inc":"For I have received … the Lord's body.","ref":"1 corinthians 11:23-29"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"The eyes of all wait upon thee, O Lord: and thou givest them their meat in due season."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"℣. My flesh is meat indeed, and my blood is drink indeed: he that eateth my flesh and drinketh my blood dwelleth in me, and I in him."},{"k":"Sequence","tx":"Sion, lift thy voice and sing,"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. John vi. 55–58","inc":"My flesh is meat … live for ever.","ref":"john 6:56-59"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"The priests of the Lord offer the offerings made by fire and the bread of their God: therefore they shall be holy unto their God, and they shall not profane his name, alleluya."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, mercifully to grant unto thy church the gifts of unity and peace, which are mystically represented in the gifts which we offer. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"As often as ye eat this bread and drink this cup, ye do shew the Lord's death till he come. Wherefore, whosoever shall eat this bread and drink this cup of the Lord unworthily shall be guilty of the body and blood of the Lord, alleluya."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we may be fulfilled with the eternal fruition of thy godhead, which in this life the partaking of thy precious body and blood doth prefigure. Who livest etc."}]},"sep-3":{"title":"Mass: Ember Wednesday in September","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 406","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Sing we merrily unto God our strength: make a cheerful noise unto the God of Jacob. Take the psalm: the merry harp with the lute. Blow up the trumpet in the new moon: for this was made a statute for Israel, and a law of the God of Jacob.\nPs. This he ordained in Joseph for a testimony: when he came out of the land of Egypt."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the frailty of our nature may be sustained by the support of thy mercy; that as in itself it continually decayeth, so by thy merciful goodness it may be renewed. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Amos, ix. 13–15","inc":"Behold the days come … the Lord thy God.","ref":"amos 9:13-15"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Come, ye children, and hearken unto me: I will teach you the fear of the Lord."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, the supplication of thy servants; that while they abstain from bodily food, their souls may also fast from sin. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Nehem. viii. 1–10.","inc":"And all the people … is your strength.","ref":"nehemiah 8:1-10"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Who is like unto the Lord our God, that hath his dwelling so high: and yet humbleth himself to behold the things that are in heaven and earth? )"},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Mark, ix. 17–29","inc":"And one of the multitude … and fasting.","ref":"mark 9:17-29"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"And my delight shall be in thy commandments: which I have loved. My hands also will I lift up unto thy commandments, which I have loved."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"O God, who hast willed to constitute thy sacrament of these fruits of the earth, vouchsafe, we beseech thee, through it to bestow upon us help both in this present life and in the life eternal. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"Eat the fat, and drink the sweet, and send portions unto them for whom nothing is prepared: for this day is holy unto the Lord our God; neither be ye sorry, for the joy of the Lord is your strength."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"Partaking, O Lord, of these heavenly gifts, we humbly beseech thee that what by thy special grace we celebrate with careful service, we may also by thy gift be worthily disposed to receive. Through etc."}]},"sep-5":{"title":"Mass: Ember Friday in September","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 408","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"Let the heart of them rejoice that seek the Lord. Seek the Lord, and his strength: seek his face evermore.\nPs. O give thanks unto the Lord, and call upon his name: tell the people what things he hath done."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, almighty God, that we who observe these appointed holy times with yearly worship, may please thee both in body and soul. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Hosea, xiv. 1–9.","inc":"O Israel, return … walk in them.","ref":"hosea 14:1-9"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Turn thee again, O Lord, at the last: and be gracious unto thy servants.\n℣. Lord, thou hast been our refuge: from one generation to another."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, vii. 36–50","inc":"And one of the Pharisees … go in peace.","ref":"luke 7:36-50"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"Praise the Lord, O my soul: and forget not all his benefits; making thee young and lusty as an eagle."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that the offering of our fast may be acceptable unto thee, and by atoning for sin make us worthy of thy grace, and bring us to the things eternal which thou hast promised. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"O turn from me shame and rebuke: for I have kept thy commandments, O Lord; for thy testimonies are my delight."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, almighty God, that rendering thanks unto thee for the bounties which we have received, we may be partakers of still greater blessings. Through etc."}]},"sep-6":{"title":"Mass: Ember Saturday in September","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 409","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"O come, let us worship and fall down: and kneel before the Lord our maker. For he is the Lord our God.\nPs. O come, let us sing unto the Lord: let us heartily rejoice in the strength of our salvation."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Almighty and everlasting God, who by a wholesome abstinence healest both our bodies and souls, we humbly beseech thy majesty that thou wouldest graciously accept our devout prayers and fast, and grant us thy help both now and hereafter. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Levit. xxiii. 26–32","inc":"And the Lord spake … your sabbath.","ref":"leviticus 23:26-32"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Be merciful, O Lord, to our sins, for thy name's sake. Wherefore do the heathen say: Where is now their God? TJ. Help us, O God of our salvation: and for the glory of thy name, O Lord, deliver us."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant unto us, we beseech thee, almighty God, that by fasting we may be filled with thy grace, and that by abstinence we may be made stronger than all our enemies. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Levit. xxiii. 39–43","inc":"In the fifteenth day … Lord your God.","ref":"leviticus 23:39-43"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Behold, O God our defender: and look upon thy servants.\n℣. O Lord God of hosts, hear the prayer of thy servants."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that thy faithful people may rejoice in the benefits which have been humbly implored; and that they who reverence thee by their outward fasting may be fulfilled with the abundance of thy good things. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"MIcah, vii. 14, 16, 18–20","inc":"Feed thy people … days of old.","ref":"micah 7:14|micah 7:16|micah 7:18-20"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Let my prayer be set forth in thy sight as the incense, O Lord. U. Let the lifting up of my hands be an evening sacrifice."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"Grant, we beseech thee, O Lord, that we may so abstain from carnal feastings, as to fast in like manner from the vices that assault us. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Zech. viii. 14–20","inc":"Thus saith the Lord … the Lord of hosts.","ref":"zechariah 8:14-20"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"Save thy people, O Lord, and give thy blessing unto thine inheritance."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"As thou grantest unto us, O Lord, to offer unto thee this solemn fast, so bestow upon us, we beseech thee, the aid of thy pardoning mercy. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Song of the Three Children, 26–66","inc":"The angel of the Lord … for ever.","ref":"daniel 3:49-50|daniel 3:47-48|daniel 3:51-58|daniel 3:61-66|daniel 3:71|daniel 3:67-68|daniel 3:74-76|daniel 3:79-87"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"Ever adore the Almighty,"},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who for the three children didst quench the flames of fire, grant, we beseech thee, that we thy servants may not be consumed by the flames of our sins. Through etc."},{"k":"Epistle","pr":"Heb. ix. 2–12","inc":"There was a tabernacle … redemption for us.","ref":"hebrews 9:2-12"},{"k":"Tract","tx":"O praise the Lord, all ye heathen: praise him all ye nations."},{"k":"Gospel","pr":"St. Luke, xiii. 6–17","inc":"He spake also … done by him.","ref":"luke 13:6-17"},{"k":"Offertory","tx":"O Lord God of my salvation, I have cried day and night before thee: let my prayer enter into thy presence, O Lord."},{"k":"Secret","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, to look favourably upon our gifts and fasts, for thou appointest the same that thou mayest be appeased thereby. Through etc."},{"k":"Communion","tx":"In the seventh month ye shall keep a feast, because I made the children of Israel dwell in booths, when I brought them out of the land of Egypt. I am the Lord your God."},{"k":"Postcommunion","tx":"We beseech thee, O Lord, that thy sacrament may perfect in us that which it containeth; that we may receive in truth and reality what we now offer under a figure. Through etc."}]},"ded":{"title":"Mass: Anniversary of the Dedication of a Church","source":"Warren, The Sarum Missal in English (1911), Part I, p. 414","note":"Source tier: English Sarum (Warren's translation of the Sarum Missal, 1911), used as the supplement because no Irish-specific text has been found. The wording is Warren's English, read from a scan of the book, so an occasional misread word is possible. Warren prints only the first and last words of each Epistle, Lesson and Gospel, so the full Scripture is shown from the Douay-Rheims. Where Warren writes 'etc.' the Missal refers back to a text given in full elsewhere.","parts":[{"k":"Introit (Office)","tx":"How dreadful is this place! this is the house of God; and this is the gate of heaven; and it shall be called the court of God."},{"k":"Collect","tx":"O God, who restorest to us, year by year, the day of the consecration of this thy holy temple, and ever presentest us again in safety at thy holy mysteries; hearken unto the prayers of thy people, and grant that whosoever shall enter into this temple to ask any blessing, may rejoice to have obtained all his petitions. Through etc."},{"k":"Lesson","pr":"Rev. xxi. 1–5","inc":"And I John saw … all things new.","ref":"revelation 21:1-5"},{"k":"Gradual","tx":"This place hath been made by God an inestimable mystery: it is without reproach."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Yf. How dreadful and adorable is this place: truly this is none other than the house of God, and the gate of heaven."},{"k":"Alleluya","tx":"Yf. The house of the Lord hath been established upon the top of the mountains, and hath been exalted above all hills."}]}};
